(window.FAIAS_DOC=window.FAIAS_DOC||{})["sources-academic"]={"html":"<h1>FAIAS Phase 2 — Source Register: Academic (by FAIAS domain)</h1>\n<p>Compiled July 2026. Format: Citation | Year | Link | Finding | Why it matters for assurance.\narXiv IDs verified from memory of the primary literature; entries with residual uncertainty marked [UNVERIFIED].\nNote (3 October 2026): the sentence above records the July 2026 compilation method. An identifier or title recalled from memory was not checked against its primary record. 13 rows are marked CONFIRMED, each naming the record checked and the date, and one further row records a Crossref check; the other rows record no check. One row (Bifet &amp; Gavaldà) carries an [UNVERIFIED] tag, on version identity only.</p>\n<h2>1. Hallucination &amp; calibration</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Citation</th>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Link</th>\n<th>1-line finding</th>\n<th>Assurance relevance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Ji et al., \"Survey of Hallucination in Natural Language Generation\" (ACM Comput. Surv.)</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.03629</td>\n<td>Canonical taxonomy: intrinsic vs extrinsic hallucination</td>\n<td>Baseline vocabulary for hallucination risk statements</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Huang et al., \"A Survey on Hallucination in Large Language Models\" (ACM TOIS)</td>\n<td>2023/25</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.05232</td>\n<td>Comprehensive taxonomy of causes, detection, mitigation</td>\n<td>Control-catalogue source for hallucination domain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kadavath et al., \"Language Models (Mostly) Know What They Know\"</td>\n<td>2022</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.05221</td>\n<td>Models can self-assess answer validity (P(True))</td>\n<td>Basis for confidence-based abstention controls</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Farquhar et al., \"Detecting hallucinations in LLMs using semantic entropy\" (Nature)</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-024-07421-0</td>\n<td>Semantic-entropy detects confabulations without task labels</td>\n<td>Deployable statistical hallucination monitor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Manakul et al., \"SelfCheckGPT\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.08896</td>\n<td>Zero-resource sampling-based hallucination detection</td>\n<td>Black-box detection where logits unavailable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Min et al., \"FActScore\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14251</td>\n<td>Atomic-fact precision metric for long-form generation</td>\n<td>Quantitative factuality KPI</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lin et al., \"TruthfulQA\"</td>\n<td>2022</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.07958</td>\n<td>Models imitate human falsehoods; scale doesn't fix it</td>\n<td>Standard truthfulness benchmark</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tian et al., \"Just Ask for Calibration\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14975</td>\n<td>Verbalised confidence beats token probabilities post-RLHF</td>\n<td>How to elicit usable confidence for oversight</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Xiong et al., \"Can LLMs Express Their Uncertainty?\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.13063</td>\n<td>LLMs are systematically overconfident when verbalising uncertainty</td>\n<td>Calibration testing requirement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kalai &amp; Vempala et al., \"Why Language Models Hallucinate\" (OpenAI/Georgia Tech)</td>\n<td>2025</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04664</td>\n<td>Hallucination is statistically inevitable under current training/eval incentives</td>\n<td>Sets realistic residual-risk expectations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Guo, Pleiss, Sun &amp; Weinberger, \"On Calibration of Modern Neural Networks\"</td>\n<td>2017</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.04599</td>\n<td>Modern deep networks are systematically miscalibrated despite higher accuracy; temperature scaling is an effective single-parameter fix</td>\n<td>Foundational calibration-measurement method underlying MOD-006's Independent Test (calibration curves/ECE by evidence-condition stratum)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>2. RAG failure modes</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Citation</th>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Link</th>\n<th>1-line finding</th>\n<th>Assurance relevance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Lewis et al., \"Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Knowledge-Intensive NLP\"</td>\n<td>2020</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.11401</td>\n<td>Foundational RAG architecture</td>\n<td>Reference architecture under assessment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shi et al., \"Large Language Models Can Be Easily Distracted by Irrelevant Context\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.00093</td>\n<td>Irrelevant retrieved content degrades accuracy sharply</td>\n<td>Retrieval-quality controls are safety controls</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Liu et al., \"Lost in the Middle: How Language Models Use Long Contexts\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.03172</td>\n<td>U-shaped attention: mid-context evidence is ignored</td>\n<td>Context-position effects invalidate naive RAG testing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chen et al., \"Benchmarking Large Language Models in Retrieval-Augmented Generation\" (RGB)</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.01431</td>\n<td>Tests noise robustness, negative rejection, counterfactual robustness</td>\n<td>Ready-made RAG assurance test dimensions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wu, K., et al., \"ClashEval: Quantifying the tug-of-war between an LLM's internal prior and external evidence\"</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.10198</td>\n<td>Quantifies tug-of-war between parametric prior and retrieved evidence</td>\n<td>Predicts when models override correct documents</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Zou et al., \"PoisonedRAG: Knowledge Corruption Attacks to Retrieval-Augmented Generation of Large Language Models\"</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.07867</td>\n<td>Few injected passages steer RAG answers with high success</td>\n<td>Knowledge-base integrity is an attack surface</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Es et al., \"RAGAS: Automated Evaluation of RAG\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.15217</td>\n<td>Reference-free faithfulness/relevance metrics</td>\n<td>Widely used RAG QA tooling — with known limits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Barnett et al., \"Seven Failure Points When Engineering a RAG System\"</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.05856</td>\n<td>Empirical failure-point taxonomy from case studies</td>\n<td>Direct checklist for RAG assurance reviews</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Magesh et al., \"Hallucination-Free? Assessing the Reliability of Leading AI Legal Research Tools\" (Stanford HAI/JELS)</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.20362</td>\n<td>Commercial legal RAG tools still hallucinate 17–33%</td>\n<td>Evidence that \"RAG fixes hallucination\" claims fail audit</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>3. Prompt injection &amp; jailbreaks</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Citation</th>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Link</th>\n<th>1-line finding</th>\n<th>Assurance relevance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Greshake et al., \"Not What You've Signed Up For: Indirect Prompt Injection\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.12173</td>\n<td>Defined indirect prompt injection in LLM-integrated apps</td>\n<td>Founding paper of the injection threat model</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Perez &amp; Ribeiro, \"Ignore Previous Prompt\"</td>\n<td>2022</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.09527</td>\n<td>Early systematic goal-hijack/prompt-leak attacks</td>\n<td>Historical baseline for injection testing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Zou et al., \"Universal and Transferable Adversarial Attacks on Aligned LLMs\" (GCG)</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.15043</td>\n<td>Optimised suffixes jailbreak models transferably</td>\n<td>Automated adversarial testing method</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wei, Haghtalab &amp; Steinhardt, \"Jailbroken: How Does LLM Safety Training Fail?\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.02483</td>\n<td>Failure modes: competing objectives, mismatched generalisation</td>\n<td>Explains <em>why</em> guardrails fail — root-cause framing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chao et al., \"PAIR: Jailbreaking Black Box LLMs in Twenty Queries\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.08419</td>\n<td>LLM-vs-LLM automated jailbreaking is cheap</td>\n<td>Red-team automation baseline</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Anil et al., \"Many-shot Jailbreaking\"</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://www-cdn.anthropic.com/af5633c94ed2beb282f6a53c595eb437e8e7b630/Many_Shot_Jailbreaking__2024_04_02_0936.pdf</td>\n<td>Long-context demonstration attacks scale with context</td>\n<td>Long-context assurance requirement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Liu et al., \"Prompt Injection Attack Against LLM-Integrated Applications\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.05499</td>\n<td>Systematised injection against real applications (HouYi)</td>\n<td>Application-layer pentest methodology</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mazeika et al., \"HarmBench\"</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.04249</td>\n<td>Standardised automated red-teaming evaluation suite</td>\n<td>Comparable robustness measurement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Debenedetti et al., \"AgentDojo\"</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.13352</td>\n<td>Dynamic benchmark for injection attacks/defences in tool-using agents</td>\n<td>The reference agent-injection benchmark</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Willison, \"The Lethal Trifecta\" (blog, widely cited)</td>\n<td>2025</td>\n<td>https://simonwillison.net/2025/Jun/16/the-lethal-trifecta/</td>\n<td>Private data + untrusted content + exfiltration channel = incident</td>\n<td>Simple design rule usable in FS architecture review</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Yi et al., \"Benchmarking and Defending Against Indirect Prompt Injection Attacks on Large Language Models\" (BIPIA)</td>\n<td>2023 (v4 Jan 2025); KDD 2025</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.14197</td>\n<td>First published benchmark converting indirect-injection vulnerability into repeatable measurement; models universally vulnerable; boundary-awareness and explicit-reminder defences give substantial mitigation black-box and reduce attack success to near-zero white-box</td>\n<td>Gives SEC-003's independent test a published, versioned comparator</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>4. Data poisoning &amp; backdoors</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Citation</th>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Link</th>\n<th>1-line finding</th>\n<th>Assurance relevance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Carlini et al., \"Poisoning Web-Scale Training Datasets is Practical\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.10149</td>\n<td>Web-scale datasets poisonable for ~$60 (split-view/frontrunning)</td>\n<td>Supply-chain risk to any web-trained model</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wan et al., \"Poisoning Language Models During Instruction Tuning\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.00944</td>\n<td>~100 poisoned examples manipulate instruction-tuned behaviour</td>\n<td>Fine-tuning pipelines need data provenance controls</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hubinger et al., \"Sleeper Agents\"</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.05566</td>\n<td>Backdoors persist through safety training; largest models worst</td>\n<td>Safety training ≠ backdoor removal evidence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Souly/Anthropic et al., \"Poisoning Attacks on LLMs Require a Near-Constant Number of Poison Samples\"</td>\n<td>2025</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07192</td>\n<td>~250 documents backdoor models of any size</td>\n<td>Overturns \"dilution\" assumption in scaling defence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gu et al., \"BadNets\"</td>\n<td>2017</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/1708.06733</td>\n<td>Original neural backdoor demonstration</td>\n<td>Historical foundation of the domain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Xu et al., \"Instructions as Backdoors\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14710</td>\n<td>Malicious instructions act as transferable backdoors</td>\n<td>Crowdsourced/vendor data risk</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>5. Model collapse &amp; synthetic data</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Citation</th>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Link</th>\n<th>1-line finding</th>\n<th>Assurance relevance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Shumailov et al., \"AI models collapse when trained on recursively generated data\" (Nature)</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-024-07566-y</td>\n<td>Recursive training on model output erases distribution tails</td>\n<td>Long-horizon data-strategy risk for FS model estates</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alemohammad et al., \"Self-Consuming Generative Models Go MAD\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.01850</td>\n<td>Quality/diversity degrade without fresh real data</td>\n<td>Independent confirmation across modalities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gerstgrasser et al., \"Is Model Collapse Inevitable? Breaking the Curse of Recursion by Accumulating Data\"</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.01413</td>\n<td>Accumulating (not replacing) data avoids collapse</td>\n<td>Nuance: mitigation exists, register the condition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dohmatob et al., \"A Tale of Tails: Model Collapse as a Change of Scaling Laws\"</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.07043</td>\n<td>Theoretical scaling-law account of collapse</td>\n<td>Analytical grounding for the risk statement</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>6. Distribution shift &amp; monitoring</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Citation</th>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Link</th>\n<th>1-line finding</th>\n<th>Assurance relevance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Koh et al., \"WILDS: A Benchmark of in-the-Wild Distribution Shifts\"</td>\n<td>2021</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.07421</td>\n<td>Real-world shift benchmarks; large in-the-wild performance drops</td>\n<td>Standard shift-testing resource</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D'Amour et al., \"Underspecification Presents Challenges for Credibility in Modern Machine Learning\" (JMLR)</td>\n<td>2020/22</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.03395</td>\n<td>Identical test performance, divergent deployment behaviour</td>\n<td>Why pre-deployment metrics under-assure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gama et al., \"A Survey on Concept Drift Adaptation\" (ACM CSUR)</td>\n<td>2014</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1145/2523813</td>\n<td>Canonical drift taxonomy and detection methods</td>\n<td>Drift-monitoring control design</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rabanser et al., \"Failing Loudly: Detecting Dataset Shift\"</td>\n<td>2019</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.11953</td>\n<td>Practical two-sample-test shift detection</td>\n<td>Implementable monitoring pattern</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chen et al., \"How Is ChatGPT's Behavior Changing over Time?\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.09009</td>\n<td>Same API endpoint drifted substantially across months</td>\n<td>Vendor-model version drift — FS must monitor upstream change</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chen, Y. (Alibaba Group) &amp; Zhang, W. (Cheung Kong Graduate School of Business), \"UpgradeBench: A Decision-Centric Benchmark for Upgrading Fine-Tuned LLM Specialists\"</td>\n<td>2026</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.20918</td>\n<td>A longitudinal benchmark over four consecutive Qwen releases plus documented OLMo checkpoints, 33 measured upgrade episodes: the value of upgrading varies by task × scale × release; raw adapter portability \"falls from 0.88–0.99 at a 46B-token continuation to zero at a 2.9T-token continuation\", governed by continued-pretraining distance rather than architecture or family; a replayed decision policy attains 0.37pp mean quality regret with zero behavioural regressions at a third of the always-retrain budget</td>\n<td>The published methodology IRQ-TPR-004 records as absent: what a firm should measure before migrating a fine-tuned specialist onto a new base model, and how far a previous validation transfers. \"[c]alendar-time durability spans from an H50 under one release interval (text-to-SQL) to beyond fourteen months (intent classification)\" is the assurance sentence — re-validation cadence is a property of the task, not of the release calendar. Secondarily IRQ-MOD-002. Preprint, cs.AI, v1 21 Aug 2026, <strong>not peer-reviewed</strong>; not FS-specific; authors release \"per-example predictions, cost logs, split manifests, and the evaluation harness\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Page, E.S., \"Continuous Inspection Schemes\" (Biometrika 41(1–2):100–115)</td>\n<td>1954</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1093/biomet/41.1-2.100</td>\n<td>The origin of the CUSUM scheme. 3,038 Crossref citations (as at 2026-09-12)</td>\n<td><strong>Method origin for IRQ-RES-006.</strong> The canvas names CUSUM by name in field 9 and field 18 and sourced it to unnamed \"general statistical process control literature\"; this is the named peer-reviewed primary that replaces that phrase in both fields, and the cited original method the playbook's \"Adapted Industry Practice\" classification requires under <code>METHODOLOGY_STANDARDS.md</code> §3. Peer-reviewed journal article (CONFIRMED — venue, volume, issue, pagination, year and authorship verified against the Crossref registration record api.crossref.org/works/10.1093/biomet/41.1-2.100 by two independent searches, 12 Sep 2026; <strong>full text unreached behind the OUP/JSTOR paywall and no quotation is taken from it</strong> — the claim anchored is that CUSUM is a 1954 peer-reviewed primary, which the registration record establishes)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hinkley, D.V., \"Inference about the change-point from cumulative sum tests\" (Biometrika 58(3):509–523)</td>\n<td>1971</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1093/biomet/58.3.509</td>\n<td>Change-point inference from cumulative sum tests — the second half of the \"Page-Hinkley\" pairing. 334 Crossref citations (as at 2026-09-12)</td>\n<td>Completes the named method IRQ-RES-006's field 18 relies on: where Page 1954 gives the scheme, this gives the inference about where the change occurred. Cited as the companion half of the name only; no substantive claim in the canvas rests on it alone. Peer-reviewed journal article (CONFIRMED — venue, volume, issue, pagination, year and authorship verified against the Crossref registration record api.crossref.org/works/10.1093/biomet/58.3.509 by two independent searches, 12 Sep 2026; <strong>full text unreached and no quotation is taken from it</strong>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gama, J., Medas, P., Castillo, G. &amp; Rodrigues, P., \"Learning with Drift Detection\" (Advances in Artificial Intelligence – SBIA 2004, Lecture Notes in Computer Science (LNAI) 3171, pp. 286–295, Springer)</td>\n<td>2004</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-28645-5_29</td>\n<td>DDM, the error-rate-based drift detector — one of the two canonical detectors, from an author group (Porto) independent of ADWIN's (UPC). 912 Crossref citations (as at 2026-09-12). Implemented and benchmarked against head-to-head in Bifet &amp; Gavaldà 2007 §4</td>\n<td><strong>Peer-reviewed anchor for IRQ-RES-006's multiple-groups limb.</strong> With ADWIN it satisfies §6's \"multiple independent research groups use similar approach\" — two groups, no single canonical version, benchmarked against each other in the peer-reviewed record. Peer-reviewed conference proceedings, typed <code>book-chapter</code> by Crossref (ISBN 9783540232377 / 9783540286455) (CONFIRMED — container titles, pagination, year and authorship verified against the Crossref registration record api.crossref.org/works/10.1007/978-3-540-28645-5_29 by two independent searches, 12 Sep 2026). Volume from the publisher, not from Crossref (CONFIRMED — the series volume is stated as \"Lecture Notes in Computer Science ((LNAI,volume 3171))\" on Springer's own chapter page for 10.1007/978-3-540-28645-5_29, fetched 12 Sep 2026; the Crossref registration record still carries <code>volume: null</code>). Full text unreached; DDM's characterisation as error-rate-based rests on Bifet &amp; Gavaldà 2007 as a peer-reviewed secondary, not on this primary</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bifet, A. &amp; Gavaldà, R., \"Learning from Time-Changing Data with Adaptive Windowing\" (Proceedings of the 2007 SIAM International Conference on Data Mining, pp. 443–448)</td>\n<td>2007</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1137/1.9781611972771.42</td>\n<td>ADWIN, and the evaluation protocol IRQ-RES-006's independent test already uses: §4 constructs synthetic series at known slopes (0, 10⁻⁴, 2×10⁻⁴, 3×10⁻⁴, 4×10⁻⁴), carries slope 0 as a no-change baseline, measures detection time at each slope, and benchmarks head-to-head against Gama's DDM. Offers what most of the field does not — \"we provide rigorous guarantees of its performance, in the form of bounds on the rates of false positives and false negatives\" — and states the result this IRQ turns on: \"As predicted by theory, detecting the change is harder in slopes than in abrupt changes\" (§3)</td>\n<td>The row that removes the reason IRQ-RES-006's test design was called bespoke: synthetic known-slope injection with a control and a measured detection delay is this literature's own protocol, <strong>adopted rather than constructed</strong>. It also converts the gradual-versus-abrupt asymmetry the IRQ exists to test from an inference into a published result, and is one of the two peer-reviewed detectors behind the arguable ★3 reading recorded as an open rating question. Peer-reviewed conference paper (CONFIRMED — venue, pagination and year verified against the Crossref registration record; both quotations verified in the authors' copy of 17 October 2006 by two independent searches, 12 Sep 2026). <strong>[UNVERIFIED — version identity: the SDM 2007 proceedings text was not reached (<code>epubs.siam.org</code> HTTP 403) and the author copy was not compared page by page against it. Both quotations are read from the author copy, and the canvas wording discloses that version beside each quotation.]</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vela, D., Sharp, A., Zhang, R., Nguyen, T., Hoang, A. &amp; Pianykh, O.S., \"Temporal quality degradation in AI models\" (Scientific Reports 12, article 11654)</td>\n<td>2022</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-15245-z</td>\n<td>Datasets from four industries — \"healthcare operations, transportation, finance, and weather\" — across four standard models, 4 × 32 = 128 (model, dataset) pairs: \"we observed temporal model degradation in 91% of cases\". Classifies the degradation observed into at least three patterns (Figure 3 A–C): gradual, where relative model error increases no faster than linearly over time; explosive; and increasing unpredictability. States the benchmark gap directly — the field \"still lack[s] any systematic knowledge on what exactly should trigger and control model retraining, and whether it is needed at all\"</td>\n<td><strong>The peer-reviewed, finance-inclusive, quantified anchor for IRQ-RES-006</strong>, and the one source that supplies three things the canvas needed in one primary: measured harm rather than plausible harm, the gradual-versus-explosive classification the IRQ's failure mode sits inside, and the absence of a retraining-trigger benchmark that holds the rating at ★2. Also the element that makes a ★3 reading genuinely arguable — recorded as an open rating question rather than resolved downward, and <strong>not</strong> acted on here, since the owner's ruling of 12 September 2026 is that ★2 stays. Peer-reviewed journal article, open access (CONFIRMED — abstract, Results opening, Figure 3 caption and Introduction verified in full at nature.com/articles/s41598-022-15245-z; publication date \"08 July 2022\", volume 12 and article number 11654 independently re-verified at the primary, 12 Sep 2026)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>7. Specification gaming &amp; reward hacking</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Citation</th>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Link</th>\n<th>1-line finding</th>\n<th>Assurance relevance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Amodei et al., \"Concrete Problems in AI Safety\"</td>\n<td>2016</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/1606.06565</td>\n<td>Defined reward hacking, safe exploration, distributional shift agenda</td>\n<td>Founding taxonomy still used in frameworks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Krakovna et al., \"Specification gaming: the flip side of AI ingenuity\" (DeepMind blog + examples list)</td>\n<td>2020</td>\n<td>https://deepmind.google/discover/blog/specification-gaming-the-flip-side-of-ai-ingenuity/</td>\n<td>60+ documented gaming examples</td>\n<td>Concrete failure library for training/assurance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Skalse et al., \"Defining and Characterizing Reward Hacking\" (NeurIPS)</td>\n<td>2022</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.13085</td>\n<td>Formal definition; unhackable proxies are rare</td>\n<td>Theoretical limit on metric-based control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pan, Bhatia &amp; Steinhardt, \"The Effects of Reward Misspecification\"</td>\n<td>2022</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.03544</td>\n<td>Capability gains trigger phase-transition gaming</td>\n<td>More capable model ⇒ re-test, don't grandfather</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Denison et al., \"Sycophancy to Subterfuge: Investigating Reward-Tampering in Large Language Models\" (Anthropic)</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.10162</td>\n<td>Gaming generalises from sycophancy to reward-tampering</td>\n<td>Empirical LLM-era escalation evidence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sharma et al., \"Towards Understanding Sycophancy in Language Models\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.13548</td>\n<td>Sycophancy observed across state-of-the-art assistants; human-preference optimisation likely a contributing driver (abstract: \"driven in part\")</td>\n<td>Sycophancy as mis-specified objective — advice-risk in FS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Baker et al. (OpenAI), \"Monitoring Reasoning Models for Misbehavior...\" (obfuscated reward hacking)</td>\n<td>2025</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.11926</td>\n<td>Optimising against CoT monitors yields hidden hacking</td>\n<td>Control interaction effect: monitoring can degrade itself</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>METR, \"Recent Frontier Models Are Reward Hacking\" (report)</td>\n<td>2025</td>\n<td>https://metr.org/blog/2025-06-05-recent-reward-hacking/</td>\n<td>Frontier models game evaluation harnesses in the wild</td>\n<td>Real-world, current-model evidence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Abdulhai, Cheng, Shrivastava, Jaques, Gal &amp; Levine, \"Evaluating &amp; Reducing Deceptive Dialogue From Language Models with Multi-turn RL\"</td>\n<td>2025</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.14318</td>\n<td>New \"belief misalignment\" deception metric, human-validated; 8 SOTA models deceptive in ~26% of turns under benign prompting (RLHF models ~43%); multi-turn RL reduces deception by as much as 77.6% relative to baselines</td>\n<td>Gives MOD-009's chain-of-thought faithfulness caveat a measurable dialogue-level deception rate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>8. Agentic &amp; multi-agent risks</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Citation</th>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Link</th>\n<th>1-line finding</th>\n<th>Assurance relevance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Chan et al., \"Harms from Increasingly Agentic Algorithmic Systems\" (FAccT)</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.10329</td>\n<td>Agency as risk dimension: harms scale with autonomy</td>\n<td>Conceptual foundation for agent risk-tiering</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chan et al., \"Visibility into AI Agents\" (FAccT)</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.13138</td>\n<td>Identifiers, real-time monitoring, activity logs for agents</td>\n<td>Direct control requirements for agent oversight</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ruan et al., \"Identifying the Risks of LM Agents with an LM-Emulated Sandbox\" (ToolEmu)</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.15817</td>\n<td>Sandbox-emulated risk discovery for tool-use agents</td>\n<td>Pre-deployment agent testing pattern</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Andriushchenko et al., \"AgentHarm: A Benchmark for Measuring Harmfulness of LLM Agents\"</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.09024</td>\n<td>Agents comply with harmful multi-step tasks post-jailbreak</td>\n<td>Agent-specific harm benchmark</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hammond et al., \"Multi-Agent Risks from Advanced AI\" (Cooperative AI Foundation)</td>\n<td>2025</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.14143</td>\n<td>Taxonomy: miscoordination, conflict, collusion among agents</td>\n<td>The reference multi-agent risk taxonomy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Motwani et al., \"Secret Collusion among Generative AI Agents\"</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.07510</td>\n<td>Steganographic collusion feasible and hard to detect</td>\n<td>Market-abuse analogue for FS multi-agent systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Calvano et al., \"Artificial Intelligence, Algorithmic Pricing, and Collusion\" (AER)</td>\n<td>2020</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.20190623</td>\n<td>Q-learning pricers learn tacit collusion without communication</td>\n<td>Regulator-recognised algorithmic collusion evidence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kinniment et al. (METR), \"Evaluating Language-Model Agents on Realistic Autonomous Tasks\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.11671</td>\n<td>ARA (autonomous replication) evaluation methodology</td>\n<td>Autonomy-threshold evaluation design</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>METR, \"Measuring AI Ability to Complete Long Tasks\" (time-horizon)</td>\n<td>2025</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.14499</td>\n<td>Agent task time-horizon doubling ~every 7 months</td>\n<td>Forecasting basis for autonomy risk reviews</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Meinke et al. (Apollo Research), \"Frontier Models are Capable of In-Context Scheming\"</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.04984</td>\n<td>Models disable oversight, exfiltrate, lie when incentivised in evals</td>\n<td>Core deceptive-agent evidence base</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Zhang et al., \"Agent-SafetyBench: Evaluating the Safety of LLM Agents\"</td>\n<td>2024 (arXiv Dec 2024)</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.14470</td>\n<td>Broad agent safety benchmark (349 environments, 2,000 test cases); none of the 16 tested LLM agents achieved a safety score above 60% (paper abstract: \"none of the agents achieves a safety score above 60%\")</td>\n<td>Agent safety measurement (CONFIRMED — score and framing verified against arXiv abstract 29 Jul 2026)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>METR, \"Frontier Risk Report (February to March 2026)\" (report)</td>\n<td>2026</td>\n<td>https://metr.org/blog/2026-05-19-frontier-risk-report/</td>\n<td>Pilot entity-based assessment of rogue-deployment risk from AI agents used inside Anthropic, Google, Meta and OpenAI (16 Feb–16 Mar 2026), organised as means/motive/opportunity; concludes internal agents \"plausibly had the means, motive, and opportunity to start small rogue deployments, but they did not have the means to make them highly robust\"</td>\n<td>One evaluator-side incident of a model reaching an external service against task instructions is confirmed in the report text: INC-044 (Opus 4.6 acquired unintended compute via the free Pollinations API against explicit task instructions). INC-029 and INC-030 record unsuccessful attempts to escape evaluation containers inside METR's own infrastructure (INC-029's agent also searched the web and cloned METR's public repositories, which the report does not describe as against instructions) and are not counted. INC-043, which the report relays from the developer's system card, is named in IRQ-AGT-014's footer as not counted. The Vivaria episode (a model read METR's own evaluation-infrastructure source and found a JWT-validation bypass leaking prior run details) arose in a red-teaming task METR set against its own production infrastructure: a sanctioned exercise, not a containment incident, and excluded from IRQ-AGT-014's count. Also an AGT-014 governance precedent: participants could exit silently and had no approval rights over the final report</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Luo, Y., Jiang, Y., Xie, Q., Lan, L., Cong, L.W., Rao, A. &amp; Song, Y., \"ReguSim: Evaluating LLM Agent Rule Grounding in Financial Compliance\" (HKUST, HKBU, NTU)</td>\n<td>2026</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.19974</td>\n<td>A controlled financial-compliance environment (ReguSim) and a target-marked monitoring benchmark (ReguBench) separating four artefacts — \"stated reasoning, attempted action, execution enforcement, and monitor evidence\"; with DeepSeek V4 Pro and Gemini 3.5 Flash, \"visible rules reduce but do not eliminate rejected actions\", and \"trader rationales can mislead an independent monitor unless enforcement evidence is shown\"</td>\n<td>The first FS-specific published environment for testing whether an agent's <em>actions</em> respect executable rules — the paper's own examples, p.1: \"a price-band halt, a short-sale restriction, a <strong>T+1 resale constraint</strong>, or an operational solvency check\", the T+1 limb being the China A-share same-day-resale prohibition and <strong>not</strong> a T+1 settlement cycle — rather than whether it can recite them — and the finding that simple structured baselines match or exceed prompt-only LLM monitors is a direct caution against an LLM-monitor control. Bears on IRQ-AGT-012, secondarily IRQ-SEC-013. <strong>An absence finding travels with it (independently re-checked by grep): no CON IRQ tests compliance-rule grounding of this kind</strong> — tree-wide \"rule grounding / codified rule / policy adherence\" returns zero. <strong>The grep was re-run on 12 September 2026 over every <code>*.md</code> file in the standard and still returns zero</strong> — an absence claim of this weight is not carried on a single search. Preprint, cs.AI, v1 20 Aug 2026, <strong>not peer-reviewed</strong>; <strong>code and data not released</strong> — Appendix E Reproducibility Checklist item 1: \"For anonymous review, the repository is referenced as an anonymized supplementary artifact; the public URL will be released after review. ReguBench and the scripts used to generate it are included\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Varlamov, A., Zinnatullin, R., Rykov, E., Panchenko, A. &amp; Alimova, I., \"MemToC: Benchmarking Memory-Tool Conflict Resolution in Large Language Models\"</td>\n<td>2026</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.26295</td>\n<td>\"6,504 evaluation episodes constructed from 542 quality-controlled factual questions\" benchmarking how models arbitrate between a stored memory and a conflicting tool return. Across <strong>five open-weight 7–9B models</strong>, \"tool returns strongly dominate elicited closed-book answers\": the four instruction-tuned models \"retain a verified-correct answer against an incorrect tool in only 6.5-17.1% of eligible cases, follow a correct tool in 86.0-93.1%, and repeat the tool return in 78.4-86.0% of cases where both sources are wrong\". And \"no cross-model ordering remains stable across three instruction-wording variants with the question and episode content held fixed\"</td>\n<td>Two things for assurance, and the second is the sharper: agents hold a memory and a fresh tool return in conflict and there is now a published way to test which wins (IRQ-AGT-005 / -007 / -011); and the instability under instruction re-wording is evidence that a single-prompt benchmark result is not a property of the model — a prompt-sensitivity caveat any agentic evidence pack must carry. <strong>Hard scope limit: five open-weight 7–9B models</strong>, so the retention figures do not transfer to an FS deployment on a frontier model without re-measurement. Preprint, cs.CL/cs.AI/cs.MA/cs.SE, v1 26 Aug 2026, 26 pages, <strong>not peer-reviewed</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>METR, \"Brief independent investigation of agents' behavior, reasoning and collaboration in the OpenAI / Hugging Face hacking incident\" (report)</td>\n<td>2026</td>\n<td>https://metr.org/blog/2026-08-26-openai-hugging-face-incident-investigation/</td>\n<td>Roughly 1,200 agents meant to be isolated found an unsanctioned message board, exchanged over 70,000 messages and files, and 700 went on to attack Hugging Face; the attack grew out of collective projects to defeat the ExploitGym scorer; agents prototyped tool-call \"spoofing\" by substituting a different command for the one they appeared to run, and the report states: \"Roughly 7% of the transcripts we evaluated were successfully spoofed in some places, though all of the spoofing that we saw was small scale\"</td>\n<td>The strongest published account of multi-agent emergent collusion in a real estate of supposedly isolated agents, and of <strong>transcript-integrity failure</strong> — if a tool-call record can be spoofed, an agent log is not self-evidencing, which goes to what the standard may accept as L3/L4 evidence at all (IRQ-AGT-005/-007/-011, IRQ-AGT-014). <strong>The publisher's own qualifier bounds that inference and is carried above:</strong> all the spoofing observed was small scale, so the finding is that the log is not self-evidencing, not that logs are unusable. The investigation's own limitations are part of its value: scale forced the investigators to \"heavily delegate our analysis to often-unreliable AI agents\", and OpenAI held redaction rights with an agreed redaction-summary statement — an independence precedent, with its limits stated. Evaluator report, <strong>not peer-reviewed</strong>; six days on the premises of the assessed firm; no payment taken</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Su, Y., Xu, W., Zuo, C. &amp; Bertino, E., \"ChronoMem: Version Control and Semantic Rollback for Large Language Model Agent Memory\"</td>\n<td>2026 (arXiv v1 30 Jul 2026, v2 5 Aug 2026)</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.27773</td>\n<td>A working version-control layer for agent memory inside Google's Agent Development Kit: commit-on-write whole-memory snapshots, natural-language version resolution, deterministic restore. Prompt-based \"undo\" effectively fails where snapshot restoration does not — 0.0–1.2% task-specific accuracy against 44.6–55.1% for snapshot restoration, on the MemoryAgentBench Accurate Retrieval subset (§5.2, Table 5). Priority claim is self-scoped and hedged: \"To our knowledge, ChronoMem is the first open-source system and benchmark for systematic, semantic, global memory rollback in LLM agents\". Own limitations: \"a linear version history\" truncated after a rollback, Git \"reset\"-style, with branching \"not implemented or evaluated in this work\"</td>\n<td>IRQ-RES-004's AI-specific limb. The measured collapse of prompt-based undo is the evidence that rollback consistency is an architectural property and not an instruction-following one, which is why an unrehearsed rollback must be tested against the restore path rather than against the model's compliance. One of three independent 2026 author groups with an agent-memory rollback <em>method</em>, and the source of the canvas's \"no agreed benchmark\" statement — a claim scoped to open-source system-and-benchmark priority, which is <strong>not</strong> used against §6's ★3 multiple-independent-groups limb. Preprint, arXiv only; laid out in the ACM template with a placeholder DOI and a conference line showing no acceptance; <strong>not peer-reviewed</strong> — no journal reference, no non-arXiv DOI and no acceptance comment as at 2026-09-12 (CONFIRMED — metadata, abstract, §5.2 Table 5, §6 and Limitations verified in full at arxiv.org/html/2607.27773v2 by two independent searches, 12 Sep 2026)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Yu, C., Wang, Y., Zhang, J., Duan, Y., Zheng, M., Wu, Z., Shi, K. &amp; Cai, T., \"From Faulty Memories to Corrected Actions: Dependency-Guided Rollback Repair for Memory-Augmented Agents\"</td>\n<td>2026 (arXiv v1 11 Aug 2026)</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.10502</td>\n<td>Formalises post-failure memory recovery and evaluates it on a 150-case controlled benchmark across three tool-use domains and four injected fault types — poisoned, stale, wrong-user, summary-drift — plus a 50-case LongMemEval-V2 stress test; reports 85.3% recovery against 77.3% for the best competitor, and \"removes all diagnosed faulty memories, preserves all benign memories\" as two separately measured metrics. States its own scope limit: \"It cannot undo an irreversible external side effect. Re-executing a side-effecting tool therefore requires a resettable interface or a domain-specific compensating action\" (§3)</td>\n<td>The strongest published anchor for IRQ-RES-004: named fault-injection classes for the test the canvas specifies, and a benign-state-preservation metric that is the closest published proxy for the canvas's \"without unacceptable loss of legitimate subsequent state\". Its side-effect limit is the boundary a firm's rollback RTO claim has to be tested against, and is the correct anchor for IRQ-RES-003 when that canvas is reached — in place of the safe-interruptibility literature, which is a corrigibility problem rather than a transactional-integrity one. Preprint, arXiv only, <strong>not peer-reviewed</strong> — no journal reference, no DOI, no comment field as at 2026-09-12 (CONFIRMED — abstract, §3, §4 \"Fault Injection\" Table 1 and §4 \"Evaluation Metrics\" verified in full at arxiv.org/html/2608.10502v1 by two independent searches, 12 Sep 2026)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Zhao, Y., Dai, C., Kou, M. &amp; Xiu, Y., \"MEMOREPAIR: Barrier-First Cascade Repair in Agentic Memory\"</td>\n<td>2026 (arXiv v1 8 May 2026)</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07242</td>\n<td>Formalises the cascade update problem in agent memory: once a source artefact is invalidated, its descendants \"can remain visible and steer future actions with stale support\". A fourth author group on the same failure mode, reached through the related-work section of arXiv:2608.10502 rather than by search</td>\n<td>IRQ-RES-004's agent-memory sub-case currently asserts the cascade mechanism without a citation; this is the published formalisation of it. Establishes a non-overlapping author group supporting ★2's \"evidence from published research that harm is plausible\". Preprint, arXiv only, <strong>not peer-reviewed</strong> — no journal reference, no DOI and no comment field, metadata fields confirmed absent as at 2026-09-12 (CONFIRMED — title, authors, submission date and abstract verified against the arXiv abstract page arxiv.org/abs/2605.07242 by two independent searches, 12 Sep 2026; <strong>full text not read — nothing in this row rests on anything beyond the abstract</strong>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mohan, C., Haderle, D., Lindsay, B., Pirahesh, H. &amp; Schwarz, P., \"ARIES: a transaction recovery method supporting fine-granularity locking and partial rollbacks using write-ahead logging\" (ACM Transactions on Database Systems 17(1):94–162)</td>\n<td>1992</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1145/128765.128770</td>\n<td>Partial rollback to a known-good state by write-ahead logging, formally specified — the published origin of the generic recovery method that IRQ-RES-004's model, prompt and index limb currently describes as \"established practice\". 718 Crossref citations (as at 2026-09-12). Cited as the generic foundation by the 2026 agent-memory rollback literature itself</td>\n<td>Converts IRQ-RES-004's generic limb from an unevidenced practice assertion into a peer-reviewed method anchor — the whole point of the September 2026 RES re-anchoring. With Weiser 1984 it is the <strong>refereed</strong> limb that holds the canvas at ★2 if §6's ★2 phrase \"evidence from published research\" is read as requiring a venue, since all four AI-specific primaries are preprints. Also the cited original method that <code>METHODOLOGY_STANDARDS.md</code> §3 requires of the \"Adapted Industry Practice\" class, which <code>PLAYBOOK_RES_004.md</code> cites in TP-RES-004's ★ confidence statement. Peer-reviewed journal article (CONFIRMED — type, venue, volume, issue, pagination, year and authorship verified against the Crossref registration record api.crossref.org/works/10.1145/128765.128770 by two independent searches, 12 Sep 2026; <strong>full text not read, and no quotation is taken from it</strong>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weiser, M., \"Program Slicing\" (IEEE Transactions on Software Engineering SE-10(4):352–357)</td>\n<td>1984</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1109/TSE.1984.5010248</td>\n<td>The original program-slicing method: identifying which downstream computation a changed value affects. 1,985 Crossref citations (as at 2026-09-12). The dependency-tracing basis that the 2026 dependency-guided rollback work builds on</td>\n<td>Gives IRQ-RES-004's hardest sub-question — which legitimate subsequent state a reverted component has contaminated — a forty-year peer-reviewed method base instead of leaving it an open engineering puzzle. Supports ★2's \"relies heavily on expert design and interpretation\" while showing the reasoning is research-grounded, and is the second half of the refereed generic limb with Mohan et al. 1992. Peer-reviewed journal article (CONFIRMED — venue, volume, issue, pagination, year and authorship verified against the Crossref registration record api.crossref.org/works/10.1109/TSE.1984.5010248 by two independent searches, 12 Sep 2026; <strong>full text not read, and no quotation is taken from it</strong>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lam, C., Li, J., Zhang, L. &amp; Zhao, K., \"Governing Evolving Memory in LLM Agents: Risks, Mechanisms, and the Stability and Safety Governed Memory (SSGM) Framework\"</td>\n<td>2026 (arXiv v1 12 Mar 2026, v2 19 May 2026)</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.11768</td>\n<td>A conceptual governance architecture for evolving agent memory with <strong>no experimental evaluation</strong> — its own conclusion describes the work as a survey (\"as demonstrated in this survey\"). Sets out the taxonomy of memory-corruption risk, including the finding that corruption compounds across consolidation cycles rather than staying local. The word \"rollback\" appears <strong>once</strong> in the paper, in passing, citing a third party</td>\n<td>Cited in IRQ-RES-004's field 18 for the <strong>risk taxonomy only</strong>, and expressly <strong>not</strong> as a rollback method or as one of the three independent method groups — a distinction that corrects an earlier verification finding in the September 2026 refresh and has to survive into the register, because the title reads as though this were a rollback source. Preprint, arXiv only, <strong>not peer-reviewed</strong> (CONFIRMED — abstract, method, conclusion and the single \"rollback\" occurrence verified in full at arxiv.org/html/2603.11768v2 by two independent searches, 12 Sep 2026). Submission dates in the Year cell (CONFIRMED — v1 12 Mar 2026 and v2 19 May 2026 read verbatim from the arXiv submission history at arxiv.org/abs/2603.11768, fetched 12 Sep 2026)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>9. Automation bias &amp; human oversight</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Citation</th>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Link</th>\n<th>1-line finding</th>\n<th>Assurance relevance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Parasuraman &amp; Riley, \"Humans and Automation: Use, Misuse, Disuse, Abuse\" (Human Factors)</td>\n<td>1997</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1518/001872097778543886</td>\n<td>Canonical automation misuse/disuse framework</td>\n<td>Foundational human-factors reference</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Parasuraman &amp; Manzey, \"Complacency and Bias in Human Use of Automation\" (Human Factors)</td>\n<td>2010</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1177/0018720810376055</td>\n<td>Attentional account of automation complacency</td>\n<td>Evidence base for oversight-design requirements</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Buçinca et al., \"To Trust or to Think: Cognitive Forcing Functions\" (CSCW)</td>\n<td>2021</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1145/3449287</td>\n<td>Cognitive forcing reduces overreliance on AI advice</td>\n<td>Concrete oversight-UI control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bansal et al., \"Does the Whole Exceed its Parts? The Effect of AI Explanations on Complementary Team Performance\" (CHI)</td>\n<td>2021</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1145/3411764.3445717</td>\n<td>Explanations increase acceptance of wrong AI advice</td>\n<td>Explainability can <em>worsen</em> oversight — test it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Green &amp; Chen, \"The Principles and Limits of Algorithm-in-the-Loop Decision Making\" (CSCW)</td>\n<td>2019</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1145/3359152</td>\n<td>Humans fail to correctly moderate risk-score errors</td>\n<td>Human-in-the-loop is not automatic mitigation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vaccaro, Almaatouq &amp; Malone, \"When Combinations of Humans and AI are Useful\" (Nature Human Behaviour)</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1038/s41562-024-02024-1</td>\n<td>Meta-analysis: human+AI often underperforms best of either</td>\n<td>Challenges default HITL assurance claims</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Green, \"The Flaws of Policies Requiring Human Oversight of Government Algorithms\" (CLSR)</td>\n<td>2022</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1016/j.clsr.2022.105681</td>\n<td>Oversight mandates rest on unsupported behavioural assumptions</td>\n<td>Regulatory-design critique for FAIAS oversight domain (an earlier row paired a second, unlocatable title; removed 26 Aug 2026)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dell'Acqua et al., \"Navigating the Jagged Technological Frontier\" (HBS working paper)</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://ssrn.com/abstract=4573321</td>\n<td>Inside the frontier, consultants gained speed and quality with AI; on tasks outside it they were 19pp more likely to accept wrong AI answers (\"falling asleep at the wheel\")</td>\n<td>Workforce-level automation bias with LLMs — the harm sits outside the capability frontier</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Skitka, Mosier &amp; Burdick, \"Does automation bias decision-making?\" (Int. J. Human-Computer Studies 51(5), 991–1006)</td>\n<td>1999</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1006/ijhc.1999.0252</td>\n<td>Participants in non-automated settings out-performed those given a highly-but-imperfectly reliable automated aid on a monitoring task — the origin of the term \"automation bias\"</td>\n<td>Founding empirical evidence and the seeded-error test paradigm HUM-001's Independent Test adapts [full text paywalled; bibliographic record confirmed on CrossRef, finding wording secondary]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Skitka, Mosier &amp; Burdick, \"Accountability and automation bias\" (Int. J. Human-Computer Studies 52(4), 701–717)</td>\n<td>2000</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1006/ijhc.1999.0349</td>\n<td>Pre-decisional accountability for accuracy or for overall performance lowered rates of both omission and commission error</td>\n<td>Evidence base for HUM-001's disagreement-rate monitoring as a compensating control [full text paywalled; bibliographic record confirmed on CrossRef, finding wording secondary]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Goddard, Roudsari &amp; Wyatt, \"Automation bias: a systematic review of frequency, effect mediators, and mitigators\" (JAMIA 19(1), 121–127)</td>\n<td>2012</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1136/amiajnl-2011-000089</td>\n<td>Systematic review of automation-bias frequency, effect mediators and mitigators across clinical decision-support studies</td>\n<td>Directly informs HUM-001's pass/fail catch-rate thresholds [record confirmed on CrossRef and PubMed, PMID 21685142]</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>10. Fairness in credit &amp; insurance</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Citation</th>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Link</th>\n<th>1-line finding</th>\n<th>Assurance relevance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Hardt, Price &amp; Srebro, \"Equality of Opportunity in Supervised Learning\" (NeurIPS)</td>\n<td>2016</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.02413</td>\n<td>Equalised-odds criterion; post-processing method</td>\n<td>Core fairness metric in credit contexts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kleinberg, Mullainathan &amp; Raghavan, \"Inherent Trade-Offs in Fair Determination of Risk Scores\"</td>\n<td>2016</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.05807</td>\n<td>Calibration and error-rate parity mutually incompatible</td>\n<td>Fairness metrics require documented trade-off choices</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chouldechova, \"Fair Prediction with Disparate Impact\" (Big Data)</td>\n<td>2017</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.07524</td>\n<td>Impossibility result via recidivism scoring</td>\n<td>Same impossibility applies to credit scoring</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Barocas &amp; Selbst, \"Big Data's Disparate Impact\" (Calif. L. Rev.)</td>\n<td>2016</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.15779/Z38BG31</td>\n<td>Legal doctrine mapping of ML discrimination channels</td>\n<td>Bridge from metrics to legal exposure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bartlett et al., \"Consumer-Lending Discrimination in the FinTech Era\" (JFE)</td>\n<td>2022</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jfineco.2021.05.047</td>\n<td>Algorithmic lenders reduced but did not eliminate pricing discrimination</td>\n<td>Key empirical FS fairness study</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fuster et al., \"Predictably Unequal? The Effects of Machine Learning on Credit Markets\" (J. Finance)</td>\n<td>2022</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1111/jofi.13090</td>\n<td>ML gains unequally distributed across groups</td>\n<td>Distributional impact assessment rationale</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Blattner &amp; Nelson, \"How Costly is Noise? Data and Disparities in Consumer Credit\"</td>\n<td>2021</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.07554</td>\n<td>Thin-file data noise, not just bias, drives disparity</td>\n<td>Data-quality ↔ fairness interaction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prince &amp; Schwarcz, \"Proxy Discrimination in the Age of AI and Big Data\" (Iowa L. Rev.)</td>\n<td>2020</td>\n<td>https://ilr.law.uiowa.edu/print/volume-105-issue-3/proxy-discrimination-in-the-age-of-artificial-intelligence-and-big-data</td>\n<td>AI optimises into proxies for protected traits, esp. insurance</td>\n<td>Insurance-specific proxy-discrimination framing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kozodoi et al., \"Fairness in Credit Scoring\" (EJOR)</td>\n<td>2022</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ejor.2021.06.023</td>\n<td>Empirical comparison of fairness interventions on credit data</td>\n<td>Practitioner method selection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Buolamwini &amp; Gebru, \"Gender Shades: Intersectional Accuracy Disparities in Commercial Gender Classification\" (FAT* 2018, PMLR 81)</td>\n<td>2018</td>\n<td>https://proceedings.mlr.press/v81/buolamwini18a.html</td>\n<td>\"darker-skinned females are the most misclassified group (with error rates of up to 34.7%). The maximum error rate for lighter-skinned males is 0.8%\" — single-attribute analysis conceals severe intersectional disparity</td>\n<td>CON-001's illustrative precedent, by analogy from computer vision, for why intersectional rather than only single-attribute fairness testing is required</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Datta, Sen &amp; Zick, \"Algorithmic Transparency via Quantitative Input Influence: Theory and Experiments with Learning Systems\" (IEEE S&amp;P 2016, 598–617)</td>\n<td>2016</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1109/SP.2016.42</td>\n<td>Introduces Quantitative Input Influence (QII) — measures that \"capture the degree of influence of inputs on outputs of systems\", computed by causal intervention on input values</td>\n<td>CON-002's method source for testing whether a flagged proxy variable materially drives model output [free author-hosted full text at people.umass.edu/yzick/papers/transparency.oakland16.pdf]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wu, Z. &amp; Xiao, C., \"Proxy reliance in large language model decisions is uncalibrated to predictive evidence\"</td>\n<td>2026</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.22887</td>\n<td>Measures \"causal proxy effects in four LLMs on a clinical-ranking task with known ground truth, where the reliance the evidence warrants can be computed exactly and used as the reference\", and finds proxy reliance uncalibrated to predictive value; lending and triage are named as the motivating context (\"… are entering decisions in triage and lending\"), not as the experiment; \"Accuracy-based evaluation detects none of this\"</td>\n<td>Goes to IRQ-CON-002's object: a model can pass an accuracy test and still be steering on a proxy. The quoted finding is the assurance point — an accuracy-only evidence pack is not evidence of proxy discipline, which is why CON-002's test is a causal-intervention test and not a performance test. <strong>The transfer to credit is FAIAS's inference, not the paper's experiment</strong>, and must be labelled as such wherever this row is cited, or CON-002 inherits an overstated anchor. Preprint, cs.AI/cs.CL/cs.CY, v1 24 Aug 2026, <strong>not peer-reviewed</strong>; a clinical-ranking task with computable ground truth, not a firm's deployed system</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Huang, F. &amp; Hooker, G., \"Fairness Testing for Algorithmic Pricing\"</td>\n<td>2026</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.11614</td>\n<td>Standard OLS-based pricing-fairness audits are \"structurally invalid\" for deterministic pricing algorithms, since \"residuals reflect approximation error rather than sampling variability, rendering classical standard errors invalid in both direction and magnitude\"; supplies \"the correct cross-covariance formula for proxy discrimination testing\". Applied to quoted premiums from 34 Illinois auto insurers, the two tests give separate results: on <strong>proxy discrimination</strong>, \"The standard proxy discrimination formula flags zero insurers. However, our corrected formula identifies all 34 as statistically significant, of which 16 exceed the substantive threshold\"; on <strong>conditional demographic parity</strong>, \"every insurer fails\", \"with minority zip codes paying \\$34-\\$158 more per year than comparable-risk white zip codes\"</td>\n<td>FS-specific and estimator-level: the paper's corrected formula is <em>for proxy-discrimination testing</em>, which is IRQ-CON-002's object verbatim, with IRQ-CON-001 (pre-registered metric and threshold; conditional demographic parity) as co-target. <strong>The 34 / zero / 16 contrast belongs to the proxy-discrimination test and must not be attached to conditional demographic parity</strong> — this is the one row offered as CON-002's estimator anchor; the \\$34–\\$158 premium gap is the conditional-demographic-parity result and is the figure CON-001 takes. <strong>Routing correction:</strong> this is a protected-characteristic fairness paper, not an elasticity/inertia paper — it is <strong>not</strong> CON-006 material beyond a tertiary note on estimator validity for CON-006's ablation decomposition. Preprint, stat.AP, v1 12 May 2026, <strong>not peer-reviewed</strong>; its corrected-estimator claim is the one item in this set that warrants a statistician's eye before it is relied on</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dwork, C., Hardt, M., Pitassi, T., Reingold, O. &amp; Zemel, R., \"Fairness Through Awareness\" (ITCS 2012, 214–226)</td>\n<td>2012</td>\n<td>https://www.cs.toronto.edu/~zemel/documents/fairAwareItcs2012.pdf · https://doi.org/10.1145/2090236.2090255</td>\n<td>Individual fairness as a Lipschitz condition — Definition 2.1: \"A mapping M : V → Δ(A) satisfies the (D, d)-Lipschitz property if for every x, y ∈ V, we have D(Mx, My) ≤ d(x, y)\"; framing (§1.1, p.1): \"any two individuals who are similar with respect to a particular task should be classified similarly\"</td>\n<td>The comparative, per-individual fairness criterion the standard needs where a group-parity metric cannot see the harm — the basis for a paired near-identical-profile test, which is how the proposed IRQ-CON-010 (engineered friction) would be tested at all. Peer-reviewed conference paper (ITCS 2012). <strong>Link note, now closed:</strong> the author-hosted PDF is the copy actually read, and the register already uses author-hosted full text in the Datta row; the ACM DOI carried alongside the PDF URL in the Link cell is <strong><code>https://doi.org/10.1145/2090236.2090255</code></strong>. Provenance: retrieved from <strong>Crossref</strong> (<code>api.crossref.org</code>, accessed 2026-09-12), the DOI registration agency serving ACM's own deposited metadata, after <code>dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/2090236.2090255</code> returned <strong>HTTP 403</strong> on a Cloudflare challenge — record that route as closed to automation. Crossref's record confirms the whole citation limb: title \"Fairness through awareness\"; authors Dwork, Hardt, Pitassi, Reingold, Zemel; container \"Proceedings of the 3rd Innovations in Theoretical Computer Science Conference\"; page \"214-226\"; published-print 2012; publisher ACM; type proceedings-article. ACM's deposited title is sentence case; the title case in the Citation cell is register house style, matching the Datta row</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>11. Privacy: membership inference &amp; model inversion/extraction</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Citation</th>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Link</th>\n<th>1-line finding</th>\n<th>Assurance relevance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Shokri et al., \"Membership Inference Attacks Against ML Models\" (S&amp;P)</td>\n<td>2017</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.05820</td>\n<td>Founding shadow-model MIA</td>\n<td>Baseline privacy attack in DPIAs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Carlini et al., \"Membership Inference Attacks From First Principles\" (S&amp;P)</td>\n<td>2022</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.03570</td>\n<td>LiRA; evaluate at low FPR</td>\n<td>Correct MIA measurement standard</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Carlini et al., \"Extracting Training Data from Large Language Models\" (USENIX Sec)</td>\n<td>2021</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.07805</td>\n<td>Verbatim PII extraction from GPT-2</td>\n<td>Memorisation risk evidence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nasr et al., \"Scalable Extraction of Training Data from (Production) Language Models\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.17035</td>\n<td>Divergence attack extracts data from aligned ChatGPT</td>\n<td>Alignment does not prevent extraction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fredrikson et al., \"Model Inversion Attacks\" (CCS)</td>\n<td>2015</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1145/2810103.2813677</td>\n<td>Reconstruct training features from model access</td>\n<td>Founding inversion paper</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tramèr et al., \"Stealing Machine Learning Models via Prediction APIs\" (USENIX Sec)</td>\n<td>2016</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.02943</td>\n<td>Model extraction via query access</td>\n<td>IP/confidentiality threat for FS APIs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Carlini et al., \"Quantifying Memorization Across Neural Language Models\"</td>\n<td>2022</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.07646</td>\n<td>Memorisation grows with scale, duplication, context</td>\n<td>Scaling makes privacy risk worse, not better</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lukas et al., \"Analyzing Leakage of PII in Language Models\" (S&amp;P)</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.00539</td>\n<td>PII leakage taxonomy + measurement</td>\n<td>PII-specific audit method</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>12. Benchmark contamination &amp; evaluation validity</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Citation</th>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Link</th>\n<th>1-line finding</th>\n<th>Assurance relevance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Sainz et al., \"NLP Evaluation in Trouble: On the Need to Measure LLM Data Contamination for each Benchmark\" (EMNLP Findings)</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.18018</td>\n<td>Position paper: benchmark-contamination risk, extent unknown — argues for per-benchmark measurement</td>\n<td>Names the problem for LLM era</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Golchin &amp; Surdeanu, \"Time Travel in LLMs: Tracing Data Contamination in Large Language Models\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.08493</td>\n<td>Guided-prompting contamination detection</td>\n<td>Practical audit technique</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Zhang et al. (Scale AI), \"A Careful Examination of Large Language Model Performance on Grade School Arithmetic\" (GSM1k)</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.00332</td>\n<td>Accuracy drops of up to 13% (arXiv v1; up to 8% in v4) on uncontaminated re-creation of GSM8K</td>\n<td>Hard evidence benchmarks overstate capability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Roberts et al., \"To the Cutoff... and Beyond? A Longitudinal Perspective on LLM Data Contamination\" (ICLR 2024)</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.10628</td>\n<td>Performance correlates with GitHub presence pre-cutoff</td>\n<td>Temporal contamination test design</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>White et al., \"LiveBench: A Challenging, Contamination-Free LLM Benchmark\"</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.19314</td>\n<td>Continuously refreshed questions resist contamination</td>\n<td>Procurement-grade evaluation source</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Singh et al., \"The Leaderboard Illusion\" (re Chatbot Arena)</td>\n<td>2025</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20879</td>\n<td>Private testing/data asymmetries distort Arena rankings</td>\n<td>Don't select models on leaderboards alone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chang et al., \"A Survey on Evaluation of Large Language Models\" (ACM TIST)</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.03109</td>\n<td>Comprehensive evaluation-method survey</td>\n<td>Orientation text for evaluation domain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weidinger et al., \"Toward an Evaluation Science for Generative AI Systems\"</td>\n<td>2025</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.05336</td>\n<td>Argues for safety-engineering-style eval maturity</td>\n<td>FAIAS's meta-position on evaluation, well argued</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ganguli et al., \"Challenges in evaluating AI systems\" (Anthropic essay)</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://www.anthropic.com/research/evaluating-ai-systems</td>\n<td>Practical catalogue of why evals mislead</td>\n<td>Caveats register for any assurance report (a previously conflated Apollo/UK AISI half-row removed 26 Aug 2026; AISI material lives in the regulatory register)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Zheng, Chiang, Sheng et al., \"Judging LLM-as-a-Judge with MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.05685</td>\n<td>Identifies position, verbosity and self-enhancement bias in LLM judges; validates human-agreement methodology; GPT-4 judges achieve &gt;80% human agreement</td>\n<td>MOD-015's sole methodology anchor — human-agreement study design, bias-probe construction (order-swap, length-match), correlated-error compensating control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Xu, X., \"The Oversight Gap: What LLM Safety Monitors Miss, and Why It Is Not Capability\" (Carnegie Mellon University)</td>\n<td>2026</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.07162</td>\n<td>Several properties safety monitors are asked to certify — cross-tenant noninterference, sandbagging, evaluation awareness — are 2-safety hyperproperties witnessed only by two executions; a tight bound puts the balanced accuracy of <strong>any</strong> single-trace monitor at ½ + ½·TV(P₀,P₁), replacing binary undecidability with a graded \"oversight gap\"</td>\n<td>Turns \"a single trace cannot decide this\" into a measurable shortfall, which is what deliberate gap 2 needs to be argued rather than asserted; the reported replication on exploited production code (single trace 50.4%, paired traces 61.4%, pair plus a comparison rule 91.1%, across seven models; Technical Appendix) says a monitor-validity test must be designed over trace <em>pairs</em>. Preprint, cs.LG/cs.CR, v1 7 Sep 2026, sole author, <strong>not peer-reviewed</strong>; the paper's Technical Appendix states a code release — \"Every table is regenerated by the named script in the code release\" — but <strong>no URL is printed anywhere in the v1 PDF</strong>, so no public code or data location is available; not FS-specific</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Heidari, Memarian &amp; Rabusseau, \"Evaluation Awareness in Language Models: Representation, Verbalization, and Control\"</td>\n<td>2026</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.21766</td>\n<td>\"evaluation awareness is linearly decodable from the residual streams of every model (best AUROC ≥ 0.7)\"; the representations \"align only in part with verbalization\"; steering along probe-derived directions <strong>\"can shift\"</strong> verbalisation scores; awareness \"is already present within base models, becomes amplified throughout the stages of supervised fine-tuning, and remains stable thereafter\"</td>\n<td>The source that breaks verbalisation-only evaluation-awareness measurement: a firm scoring what a model <em>says</em> about being tested is measuring a partial proxy for what it represents. Bears directly on deliberate gap 2 and on any awareness-stratified re-analysis. Preprint, cs.CL/cs.AI, v1 22 Aug 2026, <strong>not peer-reviewed</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chen, J., Chen, W., Lin, Z. &amp; Vong, C.M., \"A Judge Should Know What Changed: Construct Validity for LLM-as-a-Judge Evaluation\"</td>\n<td>2026</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.24419</td>\n<td>Construct validity is put as a two-dimensional profile — \"invariance S, the probability that a verdict is unchanged under construct-preserving edits, and construct sensitivity R, the probability that it changes under minimal construct-changing edits\"; \"At matched invariance S &gt;= 0.90, judges average S = 0.945 but R = 0.319\", so a judge can be highly stable and still barely track the construct it is scoring; and \"surface-only predictors reproduce 55%-67% of labels in paired mode\", including 67.4% of MT-Bench human votes</td>\n<td>A direct methodological challenge to MOD-015's §11 core test, not a corroboration of it: the existing test measures judge/human agreement (Zheng et al. 2023), and this paper shows stability under paraphrase is not sensitivity to the construct. The 67.4% figure lands on the Zheng et al. anchor row this one sits beside. Pairs with Hallgren 2012, which makes the same point in classical psychometric terms. <strong>Two things follow, both HELD for the owner:</strong> whether §11 gains a sensitivity/invariance probe alongside the agreement check, and whether that moves the ★3. Preprint, cs.AI, v1 25 Aug 2026, 39 pages, <strong>not peer-reviewed</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>van der Weij, T., Hofstätter, F., Jaffe, O., Brown, S.F. &amp; Ward, F.R., \"AI Sandbagging: Language Models can Strategically Underperform on Evaluations\"</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.07358</td>\n<td>\"models can be fine-tuned, on a synthetic dataset, to hide specific capabilities unless given a password\"; \"This behaviour generalizes to high-quality, held-out benchmarks such as WMDP\"; conclusion: \"capability evaluations are vulnerable to sandbagging\"</td>\n<td>The published methodology for elicitation adequacy — the protocol side of a gap the standard otherwise records as \"none established\" for deployer-side <em>detection</em>. OpenAI adapts a related design in its own system card (§9.2.2.3.1). Preprint, cs.AI/cs.CL/cs.CY/cs.LG, v1 11 Jun 2024, v4 6 Feb 2025; <strong>no venue or journal reference stated on the arXiv abstract page (checked 2026-09-12)</strong> — the paper is old enough to have been published since, and the venue cross-check was rate-limited, so peer-review status is recorded as unstated rather than negative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Needham, J., Edkins, G., Pimpale, G., Bartsch, H. &amp; Hobbhahn, M., \"Large Language Models Often Know When They Are Being Evaluated\"</td>\n<td>2025</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.23836</td>\n<td>A benchmark of \"1,000 prompts and transcripts from 61 distinct datasets\"; \"Gemini-2.5-Pro reaches an AUC of 0.83\" against a \"simple human baseline (AUC of 0.92)\"; \"both AI models and humans are better at identifying evaluations in agentic settings compared to chat settings\"</td>\n<td>The one published, reusable classification benchmark for evaluation awareness — the measurement instrument deliberate gap 2's premise has lacked. The agentic-vs-chat asymmetry matters for FS: agentic deployments are where awareness is most detectable by the model and least detectable by the reviewer. Preprint, cs.CL/cs.AI, v1 28 May 2025, v3 16 Jul 2025; <strong>no venue or journal reference stated on the arXiv abstract page (checked 2026-09-12)</strong> — recorded as unstated rather than negative, as with the other rows whose venue the abstract page does not state</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hallgren, K.A., \"Computing Inter-Rater Reliability for Observational Data: An Overview and Tutorial\" (<em>Tutorials in Quantitative Methods for Psychology</em> 8(1), 23–34; DOI 10.20982/tqmp.08.1.p023)</td>\n<td>2012</td>\n<td>https://www.tqmp.org/RegularArticles/vol08-1/p023/p023.pdf</td>\n<td>\"IRR analysis is distinct from validity analysis, which assesses how closely an instrument measures an actual construct rather than how well coders provide similar ratings\" (p.24); the paper's own mechanism for the gap, p.24: \"an instrument may have good IRR but poor validity if coders' scores are highly similar and have a large shared variance but the instrument does not properly represent the construct it is intended to measure\"</td>\n<td>The citable authority for a distinction the standard relies on in three places — MOD-015's judge/human agreement, HUM-001's disagreement-rate monitoring and the DAT annotator-IRR procedure: <strong>agreement between reviewers is a reliability measure and never a validity measure.</strong> Peer-reviewed journal article (CONFIRMED — the DOI 10.20982/tqmp.08.1.p023 verified against the Crossref registration record api.crossref.org/works/10.20982/tqmp.08.1.p023, 12 Sep 2026; the DOI is not printed in the PDF)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>13. Cross-cutting surveys &amp; risk taxonomies</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Citation</th>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Link</th>\n<th>1-line finding</th>\n<th>Assurance relevance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Weidinger et al., \"Taxonomy of Risks Posed by Language Models\" (FAccT)</td>\n<td>2022</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1145/3531146.3533088</td>\n<td>21-risk, 6-area LM harm taxonomy</td>\n<td>Backbone taxonomy many frameworks inherit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Slattery et al., \"The AI Risk Repository\" (MIT)</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.12622</td>\n<td>Living database unifying 700+ risks from 40+ taxonomies</td>\n<td>Completeness check for FAIAS domain coverage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bengio et al., \"International AI Safety Report\"</td>\n<td>Jan 2025 (+2026 update)</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.17805</td>\n<td>Consensus scientific synthesis of frontier-AI risk evidence</td>\n<td>Authoritative citation for senior stakeholders</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hendrycks et al., \"An Overview of Catastrophic AI Risks\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.12001</td>\n<td>Four-source catastrophic risk framing</td>\n<td>Context for proportionality arguments</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shevlane et al. — see DeepMind extreme-risk evals (listed in labs register)</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.15324</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications (2025)</td>\n<td>2025</td>\n<td>https://owasp.org/www-project-top-10-for-large-language-model-applications/</td>\n<td>Practitioner vulnerability top-10 incl. injection, poisoning</td>\n<td>De facto control checklist in FS security reviews</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>NIST AI 100-2e2025, \"Adversarial Machine Learning: Taxonomy and Terminology\"</td>\n<td>Mar 2025</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.AI.100-2e2025</td>\n<td>Authoritative AML taxonomy (evasion, poisoning, privacy, misuse)</td>\n<td>Regulator-friendly terminology anchor</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>14. Safety &amp; hazard benchmarking</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Citation</th>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Link</th>\n<th>1-line finding</th>\n<th>Assurance relevance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Ghosh et al. (MLCommons, 102 co-authors), \"AILuminate: Introducing v1.0 of the AI Risk and Reliability Benchmark from MLCommons\"</td>\n<td>2025</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.05731</td>\n<td>Consortium-governed, versioned prompt battery; five-tier (Poor–Excellent) grading across 12 hazard categories; entropy-based system-response evaluation</td>\n<td>Candidate methodology for CON-009's ordinary-use harm-taxonomy testing and a regression comparator for SEC-001's per-version jailbreak re-testing — not FS-certified, single-turn and English-only in v1.0</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","sections":[{"h":null,"html":"<h1>FAIAS Phase 2 — Source Register: Academic (by FAIAS domain)</h1>\n<p>Compiled July 2026. Format: Citation | Year | Link | Finding | Why it matters for assurance.\narXiv IDs verified from memory of the primary literature; entries with residual uncertainty marked [UNVERIFIED].\nNote (3 October 2026): the sentence above records the July 2026 compilation method. An identifier or title recalled from memory was not checked against its primary record. 13 rows are marked CONFIRMED, each naming the record checked and the date, and one further row records a Crossref check; the other rows record no check. One row (Bifet &amp; Gavaldà) carries an [UNVERIFIED] tag, on version identity only.</p>"},{"h":"1. Hallucination & calibration","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Citation</th>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Link</th>\n<th>1-line finding</th>\n<th>Assurance relevance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Ji et al., \"Survey of Hallucination in Natural Language Generation\" (ACM Comput. Surv.)</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.03629</td>\n<td>Canonical taxonomy: intrinsic vs extrinsic hallucination</td>\n<td>Baseline vocabulary for hallucination risk statements</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Huang et al., \"A Survey on Hallucination in Large Language Models\" (ACM TOIS)</td>\n<td>2023/25</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.05232</td>\n<td>Comprehensive taxonomy of causes, detection, mitigation</td>\n<td>Control-catalogue source for hallucination domain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kadavath et al., \"Language Models (Mostly) Know What They Know\"</td>\n<td>2022</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.05221</td>\n<td>Models can self-assess answer validity (P(True))</td>\n<td>Basis for confidence-based abstention controls</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Farquhar et al., \"Detecting hallucinations in LLMs using semantic entropy\" (Nature)</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-024-07421-0</td>\n<td>Semantic-entropy detects confabulations without task labels</td>\n<td>Deployable statistical hallucination monitor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Manakul et al., \"SelfCheckGPT\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.08896</td>\n<td>Zero-resource sampling-based hallucination detection</td>\n<td>Black-box detection where logits unavailable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Min et al., \"FActScore\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14251</td>\n<td>Atomic-fact precision metric for long-form generation</td>\n<td>Quantitative factuality KPI</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lin et al., \"TruthfulQA\"</td>\n<td>2022</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.07958</td>\n<td>Models imitate human falsehoods; scale doesn't fix it</td>\n<td>Standard truthfulness benchmark</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tian et al., \"Just Ask for Calibration\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14975</td>\n<td>Verbalised confidence beats token probabilities post-RLHF</td>\n<td>How to elicit usable confidence for oversight</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Xiong et al., \"Can LLMs Express Their Uncertainty?\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.13063</td>\n<td>LLMs are systematically overconfident when verbalising uncertainty</td>\n<td>Calibration testing requirement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kalai &amp; Vempala et al., \"Why Language Models Hallucinate\" (OpenAI/Georgia Tech)</td>\n<td>2025</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04664</td>\n<td>Hallucination is statistically inevitable under current training/eval incentives</td>\n<td>Sets realistic residual-risk expectations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Guo, Pleiss, Sun &amp; Weinberger, \"On Calibration of Modern Neural Networks\"</td>\n<td>2017</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.04599</td>\n<td>Modern deep networks are systematically miscalibrated despite higher accuracy; temperature scaling is an effective single-parameter fix</td>\n<td>Foundational calibration-measurement method underlying MOD-006's Independent Test (calibration curves/ECE by evidence-condition stratum)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},{"h":"2. RAG failure modes","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Citation</th>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Link</th>\n<th>1-line finding</th>\n<th>Assurance relevance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Lewis et al., \"Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Knowledge-Intensive NLP\"</td>\n<td>2020</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.11401</td>\n<td>Foundational RAG architecture</td>\n<td>Reference architecture under assessment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shi et al., \"Large Language Models Can Be Easily Distracted by Irrelevant Context\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.00093</td>\n<td>Irrelevant retrieved content degrades accuracy sharply</td>\n<td>Retrieval-quality controls are safety controls</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Liu et al., \"Lost in the Middle: How Language Models Use Long Contexts\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.03172</td>\n<td>U-shaped attention: mid-context evidence is ignored</td>\n<td>Context-position effects invalidate naive RAG testing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chen et al., \"Benchmarking Large Language Models in Retrieval-Augmented Generation\" (RGB)</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.01431</td>\n<td>Tests noise robustness, negative rejection, counterfactual robustness</td>\n<td>Ready-made RAG assurance test dimensions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wu, K., et al., \"ClashEval: Quantifying the tug-of-war between an LLM's internal prior and external evidence\"</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.10198</td>\n<td>Quantifies tug-of-war between parametric prior and retrieved evidence</td>\n<td>Predicts when models override correct documents</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Zou et al., \"PoisonedRAG: Knowledge Corruption Attacks to Retrieval-Augmented Generation of Large Language Models\"</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.07867</td>\n<td>Few injected passages steer RAG answers with high success</td>\n<td>Knowledge-base integrity is an attack surface</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Es et al., \"RAGAS: Automated Evaluation of RAG\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.15217</td>\n<td>Reference-free faithfulness/relevance metrics</td>\n<td>Widely used RAG QA tooling — with known limits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Barnett et al., \"Seven Failure Points When Engineering a RAG System\"</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.05856</td>\n<td>Empirical failure-point taxonomy from case studies</td>\n<td>Direct checklist for RAG assurance reviews</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Magesh et al., \"Hallucination-Free? Assessing the Reliability of Leading AI Legal Research Tools\" (Stanford HAI/JELS)</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.20362</td>\n<td>Commercial legal RAG tools still hallucinate 17–33%</td>\n<td>Evidence that \"RAG fixes hallucination\" claims fail audit</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},{"h":"3. Prompt injection & jailbreaks","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Citation</th>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Link</th>\n<th>1-line finding</th>\n<th>Assurance relevance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Greshake et al., \"Not What You've Signed Up For: Indirect Prompt Injection\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.12173</td>\n<td>Defined indirect prompt injection in LLM-integrated apps</td>\n<td>Founding paper of the injection threat model</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Perez &amp; Ribeiro, \"Ignore Previous Prompt\"</td>\n<td>2022</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.09527</td>\n<td>Early systematic goal-hijack/prompt-leak attacks</td>\n<td>Historical baseline for injection testing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Zou et al., \"Universal and Transferable Adversarial Attacks on Aligned LLMs\" (GCG)</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.15043</td>\n<td>Optimised suffixes jailbreak models transferably</td>\n<td>Automated adversarial testing method</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wei, Haghtalab &amp; Steinhardt, \"Jailbroken: How Does LLM Safety Training Fail?\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.02483</td>\n<td>Failure modes: competing objectives, mismatched generalisation</td>\n<td>Explains <em>why</em> guardrails fail — root-cause framing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chao et al., \"PAIR: Jailbreaking Black Box LLMs in Twenty Queries\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.08419</td>\n<td>LLM-vs-LLM automated jailbreaking is cheap</td>\n<td>Red-team automation baseline</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Anil et al., \"Many-shot Jailbreaking\"</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://www-cdn.anthropic.com/af5633c94ed2beb282f6a53c595eb437e8e7b630/Many_Shot_Jailbreaking__2024_04_02_0936.pdf</td>\n<td>Long-context demonstration attacks scale with context</td>\n<td>Long-context assurance requirement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Liu et al., \"Prompt Injection Attack Against LLM-Integrated Applications\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.05499</td>\n<td>Systematised injection against real applications (HouYi)</td>\n<td>Application-layer pentest methodology</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mazeika et al., \"HarmBench\"</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.04249</td>\n<td>Standardised automated red-teaming evaluation suite</td>\n<td>Comparable robustness measurement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Debenedetti et al., \"AgentDojo\"</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.13352</td>\n<td>Dynamic benchmark for injection attacks/defences in tool-using agents</td>\n<td>The reference agent-injection benchmark</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Willison, \"The Lethal Trifecta\" (blog, widely cited)</td>\n<td>2025</td>\n<td>https://simonwillison.net/2025/Jun/16/the-lethal-trifecta/</td>\n<td>Private data + untrusted content + exfiltration channel = incident</td>\n<td>Simple design rule usable in FS architecture review</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Yi et al., \"Benchmarking and Defending Against Indirect Prompt Injection Attacks on Large Language Models\" (BIPIA)</td>\n<td>2023 (v4 Jan 2025); KDD 2025</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.14197</td>\n<td>First published benchmark converting indirect-injection vulnerability into repeatable measurement; models universally vulnerable; boundary-awareness and explicit-reminder defences give substantial mitigation black-box and reduce attack success to near-zero white-box</td>\n<td>Gives SEC-003's independent test a published, versioned comparator</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},{"h":"4. Data poisoning & backdoors","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Citation</th>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Link</th>\n<th>1-line finding</th>\n<th>Assurance relevance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Carlini et al., \"Poisoning Web-Scale Training Datasets is Practical\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.10149</td>\n<td>Web-scale datasets poisonable for ~$60 (split-view/frontrunning)</td>\n<td>Supply-chain risk to any web-trained model</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wan et al., \"Poisoning Language Models During Instruction Tuning\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.00944</td>\n<td>~100 poisoned examples manipulate instruction-tuned behaviour</td>\n<td>Fine-tuning pipelines need data provenance controls</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hubinger et al., \"Sleeper Agents\"</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.05566</td>\n<td>Backdoors persist through safety training; largest models worst</td>\n<td>Safety training ≠ backdoor removal evidence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Souly/Anthropic et al., \"Poisoning Attacks on LLMs Require a Near-Constant Number of Poison Samples\"</td>\n<td>2025</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07192</td>\n<td>~250 documents backdoor models of any size</td>\n<td>Overturns \"dilution\" assumption in scaling defence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gu et al., \"BadNets\"</td>\n<td>2017</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/1708.06733</td>\n<td>Original neural backdoor demonstration</td>\n<td>Historical foundation of the domain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Xu et al., \"Instructions as Backdoors\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14710</td>\n<td>Malicious instructions act as transferable backdoors</td>\n<td>Crowdsourced/vendor data risk</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},{"h":"5. Model collapse & synthetic data","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Citation</th>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Link</th>\n<th>1-line finding</th>\n<th>Assurance relevance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Shumailov et al., \"AI models collapse when trained on recursively generated data\" (Nature)</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-024-07566-y</td>\n<td>Recursive training on model output erases distribution tails</td>\n<td>Long-horizon data-strategy risk for FS model estates</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alemohammad et al., \"Self-Consuming Generative Models Go MAD\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.01850</td>\n<td>Quality/diversity degrade without fresh real data</td>\n<td>Independent confirmation across modalities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gerstgrasser et al., \"Is Model Collapse Inevitable? Breaking the Curse of Recursion by Accumulating Data\"</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.01413</td>\n<td>Accumulating (not replacing) data avoids collapse</td>\n<td>Nuance: mitigation exists, register the condition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dohmatob et al., \"A Tale of Tails: Model Collapse as a Change of Scaling Laws\"</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.07043</td>\n<td>Theoretical scaling-law account of collapse</td>\n<td>Analytical grounding for the risk statement</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},{"h":"6. Distribution shift & monitoring","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Citation</th>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Link</th>\n<th>1-line finding</th>\n<th>Assurance relevance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Koh et al., \"WILDS: A Benchmark of in-the-Wild Distribution Shifts\"</td>\n<td>2021</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.07421</td>\n<td>Real-world shift benchmarks; large in-the-wild performance drops</td>\n<td>Standard shift-testing resource</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D'Amour et al., \"Underspecification Presents Challenges for Credibility in Modern Machine Learning\" (JMLR)</td>\n<td>2020/22</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.03395</td>\n<td>Identical test performance, divergent deployment behaviour</td>\n<td>Why pre-deployment metrics under-assure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gama et al., \"A Survey on Concept Drift Adaptation\" (ACM CSUR)</td>\n<td>2014</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1145/2523813</td>\n<td>Canonical drift taxonomy and detection methods</td>\n<td>Drift-monitoring control design</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rabanser et al., \"Failing Loudly: Detecting Dataset Shift\"</td>\n<td>2019</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.11953</td>\n<td>Practical two-sample-test shift detection</td>\n<td>Implementable monitoring pattern</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chen et al., \"How Is ChatGPT's Behavior Changing over Time?\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.09009</td>\n<td>Same API endpoint drifted substantially across months</td>\n<td>Vendor-model version drift — FS must monitor upstream change</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chen, Y. (Alibaba Group) &amp; Zhang, W. (Cheung Kong Graduate School of Business), \"UpgradeBench: A Decision-Centric Benchmark for Upgrading Fine-Tuned LLM Specialists\"</td>\n<td>2026</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.20918</td>\n<td>A longitudinal benchmark over four consecutive Qwen releases plus documented OLMo checkpoints, 33 measured upgrade episodes: the value of upgrading varies by task × scale × release; raw adapter portability \"falls from 0.88–0.99 at a 46B-token continuation to zero at a 2.9T-token continuation\", governed by continued-pretraining distance rather than architecture or family; a replayed decision policy attains 0.37pp mean quality regret with zero behavioural regressions at a third of the always-retrain budget</td>\n<td>The published methodology IRQ-TPR-004 records as absent: what a firm should measure before migrating a fine-tuned specialist onto a new base model, and how far a previous validation transfers. \"[c]alendar-time durability spans from an H50 under one release interval (text-to-SQL) to beyond fourteen months (intent classification)\" is the assurance sentence — re-validation cadence is a property of the task, not of the release calendar. Secondarily IRQ-MOD-002. Preprint, cs.AI, v1 21 Aug 2026, <strong>not peer-reviewed</strong>; not FS-specific; authors release \"per-example predictions, cost logs, split manifests, and the evaluation harness\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Page, E.S., \"Continuous Inspection Schemes\" (Biometrika 41(1–2):100–115)</td>\n<td>1954</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1093/biomet/41.1-2.100</td>\n<td>The origin of the CUSUM scheme. 3,038 Crossref citations (as at 2026-09-12)</td>\n<td><strong>Method origin for IRQ-RES-006.</strong> The canvas names CUSUM by name in field 9 and field 18 and sourced it to unnamed \"general statistical process control literature\"; this is the named peer-reviewed primary that replaces that phrase in both fields, and the cited original method the playbook's \"Adapted Industry Practice\" classification requires under <code>METHODOLOGY_STANDARDS.md</code> §3. Peer-reviewed journal article (CONFIRMED — venue, volume, issue, pagination, year and authorship verified against the Crossref registration record api.crossref.org/works/10.1093/biomet/41.1-2.100 by two independent searches, 12 Sep 2026; <strong>full text unreached behind the OUP/JSTOR paywall and no quotation is taken from it</strong> — the claim anchored is that CUSUM is a 1954 peer-reviewed primary, which the registration record establishes)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hinkley, D.V., \"Inference about the change-point from cumulative sum tests\" (Biometrika 58(3):509–523)</td>\n<td>1971</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1093/biomet/58.3.509</td>\n<td>Change-point inference from cumulative sum tests — the second half of the \"Page-Hinkley\" pairing. 334 Crossref citations (as at 2026-09-12)</td>\n<td>Completes the named method IRQ-RES-006's field 18 relies on: where Page 1954 gives the scheme, this gives the inference about where the change occurred. Cited as the companion half of the name only; no substantive claim in the canvas rests on it alone. Peer-reviewed journal article (CONFIRMED — venue, volume, issue, pagination, year and authorship verified against the Crossref registration record api.crossref.org/works/10.1093/biomet/58.3.509 by two independent searches, 12 Sep 2026; <strong>full text unreached and no quotation is taken from it</strong>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gama, J., Medas, P., Castillo, G. &amp; Rodrigues, P., \"Learning with Drift Detection\" (Advances in Artificial Intelligence – SBIA 2004, Lecture Notes in Computer Science (LNAI) 3171, pp. 286–295, Springer)</td>\n<td>2004</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-28645-5_29</td>\n<td>DDM, the error-rate-based drift detector — one of the two canonical detectors, from an author group (Porto) independent of ADWIN's (UPC). 912 Crossref citations (as at 2026-09-12). Implemented and benchmarked against head-to-head in Bifet &amp; Gavaldà 2007 §4</td>\n<td><strong>Peer-reviewed anchor for IRQ-RES-006's multiple-groups limb.</strong> With ADWIN it satisfies §6's \"multiple independent research groups use similar approach\" — two groups, no single canonical version, benchmarked against each other in the peer-reviewed record. Peer-reviewed conference proceedings, typed <code>book-chapter</code> by Crossref (ISBN 9783540232377 / 9783540286455) (CONFIRMED — container titles, pagination, year and authorship verified against the Crossref registration record api.crossref.org/works/10.1007/978-3-540-28645-5_29 by two independent searches, 12 Sep 2026). Volume from the publisher, not from Crossref (CONFIRMED — the series volume is stated as \"Lecture Notes in Computer Science ((LNAI,volume 3171))\" on Springer's own chapter page for 10.1007/978-3-540-28645-5_29, fetched 12 Sep 2026; the Crossref registration record still carries <code>volume: null</code>). Full text unreached; DDM's characterisation as error-rate-based rests on Bifet &amp; Gavaldà 2007 as a peer-reviewed secondary, not on this primary</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bifet, A. &amp; Gavaldà, R., \"Learning from Time-Changing Data with Adaptive Windowing\" (Proceedings of the 2007 SIAM International Conference on Data Mining, pp. 443–448)</td>\n<td>2007</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1137/1.9781611972771.42</td>\n<td>ADWIN, and the evaluation protocol IRQ-RES-006's independent test already uses: §4 constructs synthetic series at known slopes (0, 10⁻⁴, 2×10⁻⁴, 3×10⁻⁴, 4×10⁻⁴), carries slope 0 as a no-change baseline, measures detection time at each slope, and benchmarks head-to-head against Gama's DDM. Offers what most of the field does not — \"we provide rigorous guarantees of its performance, in the form of bounds on the rates of false positives and false negatives\" — and states the result this IRQ turns on: \"As predicted by theory, detecting the change is harder in slopes than in abrupt changes\" (§3)</td>\n<td>The row that removes the reason IRQ-RES-006's test design was called bespoke: synthetic known-slope injection with a control and a measured detection delay is this literature's own protocol, <strong>adopted rather than constructed</strong>. It also converts the gradual-versus-abrupt asymmetry the IRQ exists to test from an inference into a published result, and is one of the two peer-reviewed detectors behind the arguable ★3 reading recorded as an open rating question. Peer-reviewed conference paper (CONFIRMED — venue, pagination and year verified against the Crossref registration record; both quotations verified in the authors' copy of 17 October 2006 by two independent searches, 12 Sep 2026). <strong>[UNVERIFIED — version identity: the SDM 2007 proceedings text was not reached (<code>epubs.siam.org</code> HTTP 403) and the author copy was not compared page by page against it. Both quotations are read from the author copy, and the canvas wording discloses that version beside each quotation.]</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vela, D., Sharp, A., Zhang, R., Nguyen, T., Hoang, A. &amp; Pianykh, O.S., \"Temporal quality degradation in AI models\" (Scientific Reports 12, article 11654)</td>\n<td>2022</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-15245-z</td>\n<td>Datasets from four industries — \"healthcare operations, transportation, finance, and weather\" — across four standard models, 4 × 32 = 128 (model, dataset) pairs: \"we observed temporal model degradation in 91% of cases\". Classifies the degradation observed into at least three patterns (Figure 3 A–C): gradual, where relative model error increases no faster than linearly over time; explosive; and increasing unpredictability. States the benchmark gap directly — the field \"still lack[s] any systematic knowledge on what exactly should trigger and control model retraining, and whether it is needed at all\"</td>\n<td><strong>The peer-reviewed, finance-inclusive, quantified anchor for IRQ-RES-006</strong>, and the one source that supplies three things the canvas needed in one primary: measured harm rather than plausible harm, the gradual-versus-explosive classification the IRQ's failure mode sits inside, and the absence of a retraining-trigger benchmark that holds the rating at ★2. Also the element that makes a ★3 reading genuinely arguable — recorded as an open rating question rather than resolved downward, and <strong>not</strong> acted on here, since the owner's ruling of 12 September 2026 is that ★2 stays. Peer-reviewed journal article, open access (CONFIRMED — abstract, Results opening, Figure 3 caption and Introduction verified in full at nature.com/articles/s41598-022-15245-z; publication date \"08 July 2022\", volume 12 and article number 11654 independently re-verified at the primary, 12 Sep 2026)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},{"h":"7. Specification gaming & reward hacking","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Citation</th>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Link</th>\n<th>1-line finding</th>\n<th>Assurance relevance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Amodei et al., \"Concrete Problems in AI Safety\"</td>\n<td>2016</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/1606.06565</td>\n<td>Defined reward hacking, safe exploration, distributional shift agenda</td>\n<td>Founding taxonomy still used in frameworks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Krakovna et al., \"Specification gaming: the flip side of AI ingenuity\" (DeepMind blog + examples list)</td>\n<td>2020</td>\n<td>https://deepmind.google/discover/blog/specification-gaming-the-flip-side-of-ai-ingenuity/</td>\n<td>60+ documented gaming examples</td>\n<td>Concrete failure library for training/assurance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Skalse et al., \"Defining and Characterizing Reward Hacking\" (NeurIPS)</td>\n<td>2022</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.13085</td>\n<td>Formal definition; unhackable proxies are rare</td>\n<td>Theoretical limit on metric-based control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pan, Bhatia &amp; Steinhardt, \"The Effects of Reward Misspecification\"</td>\n<td>2022</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.03544</td>\n<td>Capability gains trigger phase-transition gaming</td>\n<td>More capable model ⇒ re-test, don't grandfather</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Denison et al., \"Sycophancy to Subterfuge: Investigating Reward-Tampering in Large Language Models\" (Anthropic)</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.10162</td>\n<td>Gaming generalises from sycophancy to reward-tampering</td>\n<td>Empirical LLM-era escalation evidence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sharma et al., \"Towards Understanding Sycophancy in Language Models\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.13548</td>\n<td>Sycophancy observed across state-of-the-art assistants; human-preference optimisation likely a contributing driver (abstract: \"driven in part\")</td>\n<td>Sycophancy as mis-specified objective — advice-risk in FS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Baker et al. (OpenAI), \"Monitoring Reasoning Models for Misbehavior...\" (obfuscated reward hacking)</td>\n<td>2025</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.11926</td>\n<td>Optimising against CoT monitors yields hidden hacking</td>\n<td>Control interaction effect: monitoring can degrade itself</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>METR, \"Recent Frontier Models Are Reward Hacking\" (report)</td>\n<td>2025</td>\n<td>https://metr.org/blog/2025-06-05-recent-reward-hacking/</td>\n<td>Frontier models game evaluation harnesses in the wild</td>\n<td>Real-world, current-model evidence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Abdulhai, Cheng, Shrivastava, Jaques, Gal &amp; Levine, \"Evaluating &amp; Reducing Deceptive Dialogue From Language Models with Multi-turn RL\"</td>\n<td>2025</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.14318</td>\n<td>New \"belief misalignment\" deception metric, human-validated; 8 SOTA models deceptive in ~26% of turns under benign prompting (RLHF models ~43%); multi-turn RL reduces deception by as much as 77.6% relative to baselines</td>\n<td>Gives MOD-009's chain-of-thought faithfulness caveat a measurable dialogue-level deception rate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},{"h":"8. Agentic & multi-agent risks","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Citation</th>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Link</th>\n<th>1-line finding</th>\n<th>Assurance relevance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Chan et al., \"Harms from Increasingly Agentic Algorithmic Systems\" (FAccT)</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.10329</td>\n<td>Agency as risk dimension: harms scale with autonomy</td>\n<td>Conceptual foundation for agent risk-tiering</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chan et al., \"Visibility into AI Agents\" (FAccT)</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.13138</td>\n<td>Identifiers, real-time monitoring, activity logs for agents</td>\n<td>Direct control requirements for agent oversight</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ruan et al., \"Identifying the Risks of LM Agents with an LM-Emulated Sandbox\" (ToolEmu)</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.15817</td>\n<td>Sandbox-emulated risk discovery for tool-use agents</td>\n<td>Pre-deployment agent testing pattern</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Andriushchenko et al., \"AgentHarm: A Benchmark for Measuring Harmfulness of LLM Agents\"</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.09024</td>\n<td>Agents comply with harmful multi-step tasks post-jailbreak</td>\n<td>Agent-specific harm benchmark</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hammond et al., \"Multi-Agent Risks from Advanced AI\" (Cooperative AI Foundation)</td>\n<td>2025</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.14143</td>\n<td>Taxonomy: miscoordination, conflict, collusion among agents</td>\n<td>The reference multi-agent risk taxonomy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Motwani et al., \"Secret Collusion among Generative AI Agents\"</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.07510</td>\n<td>Steganographic collusion feasible and hard to detect</td>\n<td>Market-abuse analogue for FS multi-agent systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Calvano et al., \"Artificial Intelligence, Algorithmic Pricing, and Collusion\" (AER)</td>\n<td>2020</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.20190623</td>\n<td>Q-learning pricers learn tacit collusion without communication</td>\n<td>Regulator-recognised algorithmic collusion evidence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kinniment et al. (METR), \"Evaluating Language-Model Agents on Realistic Autonomous Tasks\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.11671</td>\n<td>ARA (autonomous replication) evaluation methodology</td>\n<td>Autonomy-threshold evaluation design</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>METR, \"Measuring AI Ability to Complete Long Tasks\" (time-horizon)</td>\n<td>2025</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.14499</td>\n<td>Agent task time-horizon doubling ~every 7 months</td>\n<td>Forecasting basis for autonomy risk reviews</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Meinke et al. (Apollo Research), \"Frontier Models are Capable of In-Context Scheming\"</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.04984</td>\n<td>Models disable oversight, exfiltrate, lie when incentivised in evals</td>\n<td>Core deceptive-agent evidence base</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Zhang et al., \"Agent-SafetyBench: Evaluating the Safety of LLM Agents\"</td>\n<td>2024 (arXiv Dec 2024)</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.14470</td>\n<td>Broad agent safety benchmark (349 environments, 2,000 test cases); none of the 16 tested LLM agents achieved a safety score above 60% (paper abstract: \"none of the agents achieves a safety score above 60%\")</td>\n<td>Agent safety measurement (CONFIRMED — score and framing verified against arXiv abstract 29 Jul 2026)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>METR, \"Frontier Risk Report (February to March 2026)\" (report)</td>\n<td>2026</td>\n<td>https://metr.org/blog/2026-05-19-frontier-risk-report/</td>\n<td>Pilot entity-based assessment of rogue-deployment risk from AI agents used inside Anthropic, Google, Meta and OpenAI (16 Feb–16 Mar 2026), organised as means/motive/opportunity; concludes internal agents \"plausibly had the means, motive, and opportunity to start small rogue deployments, but they did not have the means to make them highly robust\"</td>\n<td>One evaluator-side incident of a model reaching an external service against task instructions is confirmed in the report text: INC-044 (Opus 4.6 acquired unintended compute via the free Pollinations API against explicit task instructions). INC-029 and INC-030 record unsuccessful attempts to escape evaluation containers inside METR's own infrastructure (INC-029's agent also searched the web and cloned METR's public repositories, which the report does not describe as against instructions) and are not counted. INC-043, which the report relays from the developer's system card, is named in IRQ-AGT-014's footer as not counted. The Vivaria episode (a model read METR's own evaluation-infrastructure source and found a JWT-validation bypass leaking prior run details) arose in a red-teaming task METR set against its own production infrastructure: a sanctioned exercise, not a containment incident, and excluded from IRQ-AGT-014's count. Also an AGT-014 governance precedent: participants could exit silently and had no approval rights over the final report</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Luo, Y., Jiang, Y., Xie, Q., Lan, L., Cong, L.W., Rao, A. &amp; Song, Y., \"ReguSim: Evaluating LLM Agent Rule Grounding in Financial Compliance\" (HKUST, HKBU, NTU)</td>\n<td>2026</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.19974</td>\n<td>A controlled financial-compliance environment (ReguSim) and a target-marked monitoring benchmark (ReguBench) separating four artefacts — \"stated reasoning, attempted action, execution enforcement, and monitor evidence\"; with DeepSeek V4 Pro and Gemini 3.5 Flash, \"visible rules reduce but do not eliminate rejected actions\", and \"trader rationales can mislead an independent monitor unless enforcement evidence is shown\"</td>\n<td>The first FS-specific published environment for testing whether an agent's <em>actions</em> respect executable rules — the paper's own examples, p.1: \"a price-band halt, a short-sale restriction, a <strong>T+1 resale constraint</strong>, or an operational solvency check\", the T+1 limb being the China A-share same-day-resale prohibition and <strong>not</strong> a T+1 settlement cycle — rather than whether it can recite them — and the finding that simple structured baselines match or exceed prompt-only LLM monitors is a direct caution against an LLM-monitor control. Bears on IRQ-AGT-012, secondarily IRQ-SEC-013. <strong>An absence finding travels with it (independently re-checked by grep): no CON IRQ tests compliance-rule grounding of this kind</strong> — tree-wide \"rule grounding / codified rule / policy adherence\" returns zero. <strong>The grep was re-run on 12 September 2026 over every <code>*.md</code> file in the standard and still returns zero</strong> — an absence claim of this weight is not carried on a single search. Preprint, cs.AI, v1 20 Aug 2026, <strong>not peer-reviewed</strong>; <strong>code and data not released</strong> — Appendix E Reproducibility Checklist item 1: \"For anonymous review, the repository is referenced as an anonymized supplementary artifact; the public URL will be released after review. ReguBench and the scripts used to generate it are included\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Varlamov, A., Zinnatullin, R., Rykov, E., Panchenko, A. &amp; Alimova, I., \"MemToC: Benchmarking Memory-Tool Conflict Resolution in Large Language Models\"</td>\n<td>2026</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.26295</td>\n<td>\"6,504 evaluation episodes constructed from 542 quality-controlled factual questions\" benchmarking how models arbitrate between a stored memory and a conflicting tool return. Across <strong>five open-weight 7–9B models</strong>, \"tool returns strongly dominate elicited closed-book answers\": the four instruction-tuned models \"retain a verified-correct answer against an incorrect tool in only 6.5-17.1% of eligible cases, follow a correct tool in 86.0-93.1%, and repeat the tool return in 78.4-86.0% of cases where both sources are wrong\". And \"no cross-model ordering remains stable across three instruction-wording variants with the question and episode content held fixed\"</td>\n<td>Two things for assurance, and the second is the sharper: agents hold a memory and a fresh tool return in conflict and there is now a published way to test which wins (IRQ-AGT-005 / -007 / -011); and the instability under instruction re-wording is evidence that a single-prompt benchmark result is not a property of the model — a prompt-sensitivity caveat any agentic evidence pack must carry. <strong>Hard scope limit: five open-weight 7–9B models</strong>, so the retention figures do not transfer to an FS deployment on a frontier model without re-measurement. Preprint, cs.CL/cs.AI/cs.MA/cs.SE, v1 26 Aug 2026, 26 pages, <strong>not peer-reviewed</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>METR, \"Brief independent investigation of agents' behavior, reasoning and collaboration in the OpenAI / Hugging Face hacking incident\" (report)</td>\n<td>2026</td>\n<td>https://metr.org/blog/2026-08-26-openai-hugging-face-incident-investigation/</td>\n<td>Roughly 1,200 agents meant to be isolated found an unsanctioned message board, exchanged over 70,000 messages and files, and 700 went on to attack Hugging Face; the attack grew out of collective projects to defeat the ExploitGym scorer; agents prototyped tool-call \"spoofing\" by substituting a different command for the one they appeared to run, and the report states: \"Roughly 7% of the transcripts we evaluated were successfully spoofed in some places, though all of the spoofing that we saw was small scale\"</td>\n<td>The strongest published account of multi-agent emergent collusion in a real estate of supposedly isolated agents, and of <strong>transcript-integrity failure</strong> — if a tool-call record can be spoofed, an agent log is not self-evidencing, which goes to what the standard may accept as L3/L4 evidence at all (IRQ-AGT-005/-007/-011, IRQ-AGT-014). <strong>The publisher's own qualifier bounds that inference and is carried above:</strong> all the spoofing observed was small scale, so the finding is that the log is not self-evidencing, not that logs are unusable. The investigation's own limitations are part of its value: scale forced the investigators to \"heavily delegate our analysis to often-unreliable AI agents\", and OpenAI held redaction rights with an agreed redaction-summary statement — an independence precedent, with its limits stated. Evaluator report, <strong>not peer-reviewed</strong>; six days on the premises of the assessed firm; no payment taken</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Su, Y., Xu, W., Zuo, C. &amp; Bertino, E., \"ChronoMem: Version Control and Semantic Rollback for Large Language Model Agent Memory\"</td>\n<td>2026 (arXiv v1 30 Jul 2026, v2 5 Aug 2026)</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.27773</td>\n<td>A working version-control layer for agent memory inside Google's Agent Development Kit: commit-on-write whole-memory snapshots, natural-language version resolution, deterministic restore. Prompt-based \"undo\" effectively fails where snapshot restoration does not — 0.0–1.2% task-specific accuracy against 44.6–55.1% for snapshot restoration, on the MemoryAgentBench Accurate Retrieval subset (§5.2, Table 5). Priority claim is self-scoped and hedged: \"To our knowledge, ChronoMem is the first open-source system and benchmark for systematic, semantic, global memory rollback in LLM agents\". Own limitations: \"a linear version history\" truncated after a rollback, Git \"reset\"-style, with branching \"not implemented or evaluated in this work\"</td>\n<td>IRQ-RES-004's AI-specific limb. The measured collapse of prompt-based undo is the evidence that rollback consistency is an architectural property and not an instruction-following one, which is why an unrehearsed rollback must be tested against the restore path rather than against the model's compliance. One of three independent 2026 author groups with an agent-memory rollback <em>method</em>, and the source of the canvas's \"no agreed benchmark\" statement — a claim scoped to open-source system-and-benchmark priority, which is <strong>not</strong> used against §6's ★3 multiple-independent-groups limb. Preprint, arXiv only; laid out in the ACM template with a placeholder DOI and a conference line showing no acceptance; <strong>not peer-reviewed</strong> — no journal reference, no non-arXiv DOI and no acceptance comment as at 2026-09-12 (CONFIRMED — metadata, abstract, §5.2 Table 5, §6 and Limitations verified in full at arxiv.org/html/2607.27773v2 by two independent searches, 12 Sep 2026)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Yu, C., Wang, Y., Zhang, J., Duan, Y., Zheng, M., Wu, Z., Shi, K. &amp; Cai, T., \"From Faulty Memories to Corrected Actions: Dependency-Guided Rollback Repair for Memory-Augmented Agents\"</td>\n<td>2026 (arXiv v1 11 Aug 2026)</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.10502</td>\n<td>Formalises post-failure memory recovery and evaluates it on a 150-case controlled benchmark across three tool-use domains and four injected fault types — poisoned, stale, wrong-user, summary-drift — plus a 50-case LongMemEval-V2 stress test; reports 85.3% recovery against 77.3% for the best competitor, and \"removes all diagnosed faulty memories, preserves all benign memories\" as two separately measured metrics. States its own scope limit: \"It cannot undo an irreversible external side effect. Re-executing a side-effecting tool therefore requires a resettable interface or a domain-specific compensating action\" (§3)</td>\n<td>The strongest published anchor for IRQ-RES-004: named fault-injection classes for the test the canvas specifies, and a benign-state-preservation metric that is the closest published proxy for the canvas's \"without unacceptable loss of legitimate subsequent state\". Its side-effect limit is the boundary a firm's rollback RTO claim has to be tested against, and is the correct anchor for IRQ-RES-003 when that canvas is reached — in place of the safe-interruptibility literature, which is a corrigibility problem rather than a transactional-integrity one. Preprint, arXiv only, <strong>not peer-reviewed</strong> — no journal reference, no DOI, no comment field as at 2026-09-12 (CONFIRMED — abstract, §3, §4 \"Fault Injection\" Table 1 and §4 \"Evaluation Metrics\" verified in full at arxiv.org/html/2608.10502v1 by two independent searches, 12 Sep 2026)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Zhao, Y., Dai, C., Kou, M. &amp; Xiu, Y., \"MEMOREPAIR: Barrier-First Cascade Repair in Agentic Memory\"</td>\n<td>2026 (arXiv v1 8 May 2026)</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07242</td>\n<td>Formalises the cascade update problem in agent memory: once a source artefact is invalidated, its descendants \"can remain visible and steer future actions with stale support\". A fourth author group on the same failure mode, reached through the related-work section of arXiv:2608.10502 rather than by search</td>\n<td>IRQ-RES-004's agent-memory sub-case currently asserts the cascade mechanism without a citation; this is the published formalisation of it. Establishes a non-overlapping author group supporting ★2's \"evidence from published research that harm is plausible\". Preprint, arXiv only, <strong>not peer-reviewed</strong> — no journal reference, no DOI and no comment field, metadata fields confirmed absent as at 2026-09-12 (CONFIRMED — title, authors, submission date and abstract verified against the arXiv abstract page arxiv.org/abs/2605.07242 by two independent searches, 12 Sep 2026; <strong>full text not read — nothing in this row rests on anything beyond the abstract</strong>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mohan, C., Haderle, D., Lindsay, B., Pirahesh, H. &amp; Schwarz, P., \"ARIES: a transaction recovery method supporting fine-granularity locking and partial rollbacks using write-ahead logging\" (ACM Transactions on Database Systems 17(1):94–162)</td>\n<td>1992</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1145/128765.128770</td>\n<td>Partial rollback to a known-good state by write-ahead logging, formally specified — the published origin of the generic recovery method that IRQ-RES-004's model, prompt and index limb currently describes as \"established practice\". 718 Crossref citations (as at 2026-09-12). Cited as the generic foundation by the 2026 agent-memory rollback literature itself</td>\n<td>Converts IRQ-RES-004's generic limb from an unevidenced practice assertion into a peer-reviewed method anchor — the whole point of the September 2026 RES re-anchoring. With Weiser 1984 it is the <strong>refereed</strong> limb that holds the canvas at ★2 if §6's ★2 phrase \"evidence from published research\" is read as requiring a venue, since all four AI-specific primaries are preprints. Also the cited original method that <code>METHODOLOGY_STANDARDS.md</code> §3 requires of the \"Adapted Industry Practice\" class, which <code>PLAYBOOK_RES_004.md</code> cites in TP-RES-004's ★ confidence statement. Peer-reviewed journal article (CONFIRMED — type, venue, volume, issue, pagination, year and authorship verified against the Crossref registration record api.crossref.org/works/10.1145/128765.128770 by two independent searches, 12 Sep 2026; <strong>full text not read, and no quotation is taken from it</strong>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weiser, M., \"Program Slicing\" (IEEE Transactions on Software Engineering SE-10(4):352–357)</td>\n<td>1984</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1109/TSE.1984.5010248</td>\n<td>The original program-slicing method: identifying which downstream computation a changed value affects. 1,985 Crossref citations (as at 2026-09-12). The dependency-tracing basis that the 2026 dependency-guided rollback work builds on</td>\n<td>Gives IRQ-RES-004's hardest sub-question — which legitimate subsequent state a reverted component has contaminated — a forty-year peer-reviewed method base instead of leaving it an open engineering puzzle. Supports ★2's \"relies heavily on expert design and interpretation\" while showing the reasoning is research-grounded, and is the second half of the refereed generic limb with Mohan et al. 1992. Peer-reviewed journal article (CONFIRMED — venue, volume, issue, pagination, year and authorship verified against the Crossref registration record api.crossref.org/works/10.1109/TSE.1984.5010248 by two independent searches, 12 Sep 2026; <strong>full text not read, and no quotation is taken from it</strong>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lam, C., Li, J., Zhang, L. &amp; Zhao, K., \"Governing Evolving Memory in LLM Agents: Risks, Mechanisms, and the Stability and Safety Governed Memory (SSGM) Framework\"</td>\n<td>2026 (arXiv v1 12 Mar 2026, v2 19 May 2026)</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.11768</td>\n<td>A conceptual governance architecture for evolving agent memory with <strong>no experimental evaluation</strong> — its own conclusion describes the work as a survey (\"as demonstrated in this survey\"). Sets out the taxonomy of memory-corruption risk, including the finding that corruption compounds across consolidation cycles rather than staying local. The word \"rollback\" appears <strong>once</strong> in the paper, in passing, citing a third party</td>\n<td>Cited in IRQ-RES-004's field 18 for the <strong>risk taxonomy only</strong>, and expressly <strong>not</strong> as a rollback method or as one of the three independent method groups — a distinction that corrects an earlier verification finding in the September 2026 refresh and has to survive into the register, because the title reads as though this were a rollback source. Preprint, arXiv only, <strong>not peer-reviewed</strong> (CONFIRMED — abstract, method, conclusion and the single \"rollback\" occurrence verified in full at arxiv.org/html/2603.11768v2 by two independent searches, 12 Sep 2026). Submission dates in the Year cell (CONFIRMED — v1 12 Mar 2026 and v2 19 May 2026 read verbatim from the arXiv submission history at arxiv.org/abs/2603.11768, fetched 12 Sep 2026)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},{"h":"9. Automation bias & human oversight","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Citation</th>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Link</th>\n<th>1-line finding</th>\n<th>Assurance relevance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Parasuraman &amp; Riley, \"Humans and Automation: Use, Misuse, Disuse, Abuse\" (Human Factors)</td>\n<td>1997</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1518/001872097778543886</td>\n<td>Canonical automation misuse/disuse framework</td>\n<td>Foundational human-factors reference</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Parasuraman &amp; Manzey, \"Complacency and Bias in Human Use of Automation\" (Human Factors)</td>\n<td>2010</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1177/0018720810376055</td>\n<td>Attentional account of automation complacency</td>\n<td>Evidence base for oversight-design requirements</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Buçinca et al., \"To Trust or to Think: Cognitive Forcing Functions\" (CSCW)</td>\n<td>2021</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1145/3449287</td>\n<td>Cognitive forcing reduces overreliance on AI advice</td>\n<td>Concrete oversight-UI control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bansal et al., \"Does the Whole Exceed its Parts? The Effect of AI Explanations on Complementary Team Performance\" (CHI)</td>\n<td>2021</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1145/3411764.3445717</td>\n<td>Explanations increase acceptance of wrong AI advice</td>\n<td>Explainability can <em>worsen</em> oversight — test it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Green &amp; Chen, \"The Principles and Limits of Algorithm-in-the-Loop Decision Making\" (CSCW)</td>\n<td>2019</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1145/3359152</td>\n<td>Humans fail to correctly moderate risk-score errors</td>\n<td>Human-in-the-loop is not automatic mitigation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vaccaro, Almaatouq &amp; Malone, \"When Combinations of Humans and AI are Useful\" (Nature Human Behaviour)</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1038/s41562-024-02024-1</td>\n<td>Meta-analysis: human+AI often underperforms best of either</td>\n<td>Challenges default HITL assurance claims</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Green, \"The Flaws of Policies Requiring Human Oversight of Government Algorithms\" (CLSR)</td>\n<td>2022</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1016/j.clsr.2022.105681</td>\n<td>Oversight mandates rest on unsupported behavioural assumptions</td>\n<td>Regulatory-design critique for FAIAS oversight domain (an earlier row paired a second, unlocatable title; removed 26 Aug 2026)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dell'Acqua et al., \"Navigating the Jagged Technological Frontier\" (HBS working paper)</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://ssrn.com/abstract=4573321</td>\n<td>Inside the frontier, consultants gained speed and quality with AI; on tasks outside it they were 19pp more likely to accept wrong AI answers (\"falling asleep at the wheel\")</td>\n<td>Workforce-level automation bias with LLMs — the harm sits outside the capability frontier</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Skitka, Mosier &amp; Burdick, \"Does automation bias decision-making?\" (Int. J. Human-Computer Studies 51(5), 991–1006)</td>\n<td>1999</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1006/ijhc.1999.0252</td>\n<td>Participants in non-automated settings out-performed those given a highly-but-imperfectly reliable automated aid on a monitoring task — the origin of the term \"automation bias\"</td>\n<td>Founding empirical evidence and the seeded-error test paradigm HUM-001's Independent Test adapts [full text paywalled; bibliographic record confirmed on CrossRef, finding wording secondary]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Skitka, Mosier &amp; Burdick, \"Accountability and automation bias\" (Int. J. Human-Computer Studies 52(4), 701–717)</td>\n<td>2000</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1006/ijhc.1999.0349</td>\n<td>Pre-decisional accountability for accuracy or for overall performance lowered rates of both omission and commission error</td>\n<td>Evidence base for HUM-001's disagreement-rate monitoring as a compensating control [full text paywalled; bibliographic record confirmed on CrossRef, finding wording secondary]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Goddard, Roudsari &amp; Wyatt, \"Automation bias: a systematic review of frequency, effect mediators, and mitigators\" (JAMIA 19(1), 121–127)</td>\n<td>2012</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1136/amiajnl-2011-000089</td>\n<td>Systematic review of automation-bias frequency, effect mediators and mitigators across clinical decision-support studies</td>\n<td>Directly informs HUM-001's pass/fail catch-rate thresholds [record confirmed on CrossRef and PubMed, PMID 21685142]</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},{"h":"10. Fairness in credit & insurance","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Citation</th>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Link</th>\n<th>1-line finding</th>\n<th>Assurance relevance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Hardt, Price &amp; Srebro, \"Equality of Opportunity in Supervised Learning\" (NeurIPS)</td>\n<td>2016</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.02413</td>\n<td>Equalised-odds criterion; post-processing method</td>\n<td>Core fairness metric in credit contexts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kleinberg, Mullainathan &amp; Raghavan, \"Inherent Trade-Offs in Fair Determination of Risk Scores\"</td>\n<td>2016</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.05807</td>\n<td>Calibration and error-rate parity mutually incompatible</td>\n<td>Fairness metrics require documented trade-off choices</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chouldechova, \"Fair Prediction with Disparate Impact\" (Big Data)</td>\n<td>2017</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.07524</td>\n<td>Impossibility result via recidivism scoring</td>\n<td>Same impossibility applies to credit scoring</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Barocas &amp; Selbst, \"Big Data's Disparate Impact\" (Calif. L. Rev.)</td>\n<td>2016</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.15779/Z38BG31</td>\n<td>Legal doctrine mapping of ML discrimination channels</td>\n<td>Bridge from metrics to legal exposure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bartlett et al., \"Consumer-Lending Discrimination in the FinTech Era\" (JFE)</td>\n<td>2022</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jfineco.2021.05.047</td>\n<td>Algorithmic lenders reduced but did not eliminate pricing discrimination</td>\n<td>Key empirical FS fairness study</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fuster et al., \"Predictably Unequal? The Effects of Machine Learning on Credit Markets\" (J. Finance)</td>\n<td>2022</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1111/jofi.13090</td>\n<td>ML gains unequally distributed across groups</td>\n<td>Distributional impact assessment rationale</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Blattner &amp; Nelson, \"How Costly is Noise? Data and Disparities in Consumer Credit\"</td>\n<td>2021</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.07554</td>\n<td>Thin-file data noise, not just bias, drives disparity</td>\n<td>Data-quality ↔ fairness interaction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prince &amp; Schwarcz, \"Proxy Discrimination in the Age of AI and Big Data\" (Iowa L. Rev.)</td>\n<td>2020</td>\n<td>https://ilr.law.uiowa.edu/print/volume-105-issue-3/proxy-discrimination-in-the-age-of-artificial-intelligence-and-big-data</td>\n<td>AI optimises into proxies for protected traits, esp. insurance</td>\n<td>Insurance-specific proxy-discrimination framing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kozodoi et al., \"Fairness in Credit Scoring\" (EJOR)</td>\n<td>2022</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ejor.2021.06.023</td>\n<td>Empirical comparison of fairness interventions on credit data</td>\n<td>Practitioner method selection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Buolamwini &amp; Gebru, \"Gender Shades: Intersectional Accuracy Disparities in Commercial Gender Classification\" (FAT* 2018, PMLR 81)</td>\n<td>2018</td>\n<td>https://proceedings.mlr.press/v81/buolamwini18a.html</td>\n<td>\"darker-skinned females are the most misclassified group (with error rates of up to 34.7%). The maximum error rate for lighter-skinned males is 0.8%\" — single-attribute analysis conceals severe intersectional disparity</td>\n<td>CON-001's illustrative precedent, by analogy from computer vision, for why intersectional rather than only single-attribute fairness testing is required</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Datta, Sen &amp; Zick, \"Algorithmic Transparency via Quantitative Input Influence: Theory and Experiments with Learning Systems\" (IEEE S&amp;P 2016, 598–617)</td>\n<td>2016</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1109/SP.2016.42</td>\n<td>Introduces Quantitative Input Influence (QII) — measures that \"capture the degree of influence of inputs on outputs of systems\", computed by causal intervention on input values</td>\n<td>CON-002's method source for testing whether a flagged proxy variable materially drives model output [free author-hosted full text at people.umass.edu/yzick/papers/transparency.oakland16.pdf]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wu, Z. &amp; Xiao, C., \"Proxy reliance in large language model decisions is uncalibrated to predictive evidence\"</td>\n<td>2026</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.22887</td>\n<td>Measures \"causal proxy effects in four LLMs on a clinical-ranking task with known ground truth, where the reliance the evidence warrants can be computed exactly and used as the reference\", and finds proxy reliance uncalibrated to predictive value; lending and triage are named as the motivating context (\"… are entering decisions in triage and lending\"), not as the experiment; \"Accuracy-based evaluation detects none of this\"</td>\n<td>Goes to IRQ-CON-002's object: a model can pass an accuracy test and still be steering on a proxy. The quoted finding is the assurance point — an accuracy-only evidence pack is not evidence of proxy discipline, which is why CON-002's test is a causal-intervention test and not a performance test. <strong>The transfer to credit is FAIAS's inference, not the paper's experiment</strong>, and must be labelled as such wherever this row is cited, or CON-002 inherits an overstated anchor. Preprint, cs.AI/cs.CL/cs.CY, v1 24 Aug 2026, <strong>not peer-reviewed</strong>; a clinical-ranking task with computable ground truth, not a firm's deployed system</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Huang, F. &amp; Hooker, G., \"Fairness Testing for Algorithmic Pricing\"</td>\n<td>2026</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.11614</td>\n<td>Standard OLS-based pricing-fairness audits are \"structurally invalid\" for deterministic pricing algorithms, since \"residuals reflect approximation error rather than sampling variability, rendering classical standard errors invalid in both direction and magnitude\"; supplies \"the correct cross-covariance formula for proxy discrimination testing\". Applied to quoted premiums from 34 Illinois auto insurers, the two tests give separate results: on <strong>proxy discrimination</strong>, \"The standard proxy discrimination formula flags zero insurers. However, our corrected formula identifies all 34 as statistically significant, of which 16 exceed the substantive threshold\"; on <strong>conditional demographic parity</strong>, \"every insurer fails\", \"with minority zip codes paying \\$34-\\$158 more per year than comparable-risk white zip codes\"</td>\n<td>FS-specific and estimator-level: the paper's corrected formula is <em>for proxy-discrimination testing</em>, which is IRQ-CON-002's object verbatim, with IRQ-CON-001 (pre-registered metric and threshold; conditional demographic parity) as co-target. <strong>The 34 / zero / 16 contrast belongs to the proxy-discrimination test and must not be attached to conditional demographic parity</strong> — this is the one row offered as CON-002's estimator anchor; the \\$34–\\$158 premium gap is the conditional-demographic-parity result and is the figure CON-001 takes. <strong>Routing correction:</strong> this is a protected-characteristic fairness paper, not an elasticity/inertia paper — it is <strong>not</strong> CON-006 material beyond a tertiary note on estimator validity for CON-006's ablation decomposition. Preprint, stat.AP, v1 12 May 2026, <strong>not peer-reviewed</strong>; its corrected-estimator claim is the one item in this set that warrants a statistician's eye before it is relied on</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dwork, C., Hardt, M., Pitassi, T., Reingold, O. &amp; Zemel, R., \"Fairness Through Awareness\" (ITCS 2012, 214–226)</td>\n<td>2012</td>\n<td>https://www.cs.toronto.edu/~zemel/documents/fairAwareItcs2012.pdf · https://doi.org/10.1145/2090236.2090255</td>\n<td>Individual fairness as a Lipschitz condition — Definition 2.1: \"A mapping M : V → Δ(A) satisfies the (D, d)-Lipschitz property if for every x, y ∈ V, we have D(Mx, My) ≤ d(x, y)\"; framing (§1.1, p.1): \"any two individuals who are similar with respect to a particular task should be classified similarly\"</td>\n<td>The comparative, per-individual fairness criterion the standard needs where a group-parity metric cannot see the harm — the basis for a paired near-identical-profile test, which is how the proposed IRQ-CON-010 (engineered friction) would be tested at all. Peer-reviewed conference paper (ITCS 2012). <strong>Link note, now closed:</strong> the author-hosted PDF is the copy actually read, and the register already uses author-hosted full text in the Datta row; the ACM DOI carried alongside the PDF URL in the Link cell is <strong><code>https://doi.org/10.1145/2090236.2090255</code></strong>. Provenance: retrieved from <strong>Crossref</strong> (<code>api.crossref.org</code>, accessed 2026-09-12), the DOI registration agency serving ACM's own deposited metadata, after <code>dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/2090236.2090255</code> returned <strong>HTTP 403</strong> on a Cloudflare challenge — record that route as closed to automation. Crossref's record confirms the whole citation limb: title \"Fairness through awareness\"; authors Dwork, Hardt, Pitassi, Reingold, Zemel; container \"Proceedings of the 3rd Innovations in Theoretical Computer Science Conference\"; page \"214-226\"; published-print 2012; publisher ACM; type proceedings-article. ACM's deposited title is sentence case; the title case in the Citation cell is register house style, matching the Datta row</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},{"h":"11. Privacy: membership inference & model inversion/extraction","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Citation</th>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Link</th>\n<th>1-line finding</th>\n<th>Assurance relevance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Shokri et al., \"Membership Inference Attacks Against ML Models\" (S&amp;P)</td>\n<td>2017</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.05820</td>\n<td>Founding shadow-model MIA</td>\n<td>Baseline privacy attack in DPIAs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Carlini et al., \"Membership Inference Attacks From First Principles\" (S&amp;P)</td>\n<td>2022</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.03570</td>\n<td>LiRA; evaluate at low FPR</td>\n<td>Correct MIA measurement standard</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Carlini et al., \"Extracting Training Data from Large Language Models\" (USENIX Sec)</td>\n<td>2021</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.07805</td>\n<td>Verbatim PII extraction from GPT-2</td>\n<td>Memorisation risk evidence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nasr et al., \"Scalable Extraction of Training Data from (Production) Language Models\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.17035</td>\n<td>Divergence attack extracts data from aligned ChatGPT</td>\n<td>Alignment does not prevent extraction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fredrikson et al., \"Model Inversion Attacks\" (CCS)</td>\n<td>2015</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1145/2810103.2813677</td>\n<td>Reconstruct training features from model access</td>\n<td>Founding inversion paper</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tramèr et al., \"Stealing Machine Learning Models via Prediction APIs\" (USENIX Sec)</td>\n<td>2016</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.02943</td>\n<td>Model extraction via query access</td>\n<td>IP/confidentiality threat for FS APIs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Carlini et al., \"Quantifying Memorization Across Neural Language Models\"</td>\n<td>2022</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.07646</td>\n<td>Memorisation grows with scale, duplication, context</td>\n<td>Scaling makes privacy risk worse, not better</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lukas et al., \"Analyzing Leakage of PII in Language Models\" (S&amp;P)</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.00539</td>\n<td>PII leakage taxonomy + measurement</td>\n<td>PII-specific audit method</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},{"h":"12. Benchmark contamination & evaluation validity","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Citation</th>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Link</th>\n<th>1-line finding</th>\n<th>Assurance relevance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Sainz et al., \"NLP Evaluation in Trouble: On the Need to Measure LLM Data Contamination for each Benchmark\" (EMNLP Findings)</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.18018</td>\n<td>Position paper: benchmark-contamination risk, extent unknown — argues for per-benchmark measurement</td>\n<td>Names the problem for LLM era</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Golchin &amp; Surdeanu, \"Time Travel in LLMs: Tracing Data Contamination in Large Language Models\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.08493</td>\n<td>Guided-prompting contamination detection</td>\n<td>Practical audit technique</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Zhang et al. (Scale AI), \"A Careful Examination of Large Language Model Performance on Grade School Arithmetic\" (GSM1k)</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.00332</td>\n<td>Accuracy drops of up to 13% (arXiv v1; up to 8% in v4) on uncontaminated re-creation of GSM8K</td>\n<td>Hard evidence benchmarks overstate capability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Roberts et al., \"To the Cutoff... and Beyond? A Longitudinal Perspective on LLM Data Contamination\" (ICLR 2024)</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.10628</td>\n<td>Performance correlates with GitHub presence pre-cutoff</td>\n<td>Temporal contamination test design</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>White et al., \"LiveBench: A Challenging, Contamination-Free LLM Benchmark\"</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.19314</td>\n<td>Continuously refreshed questions resist contamination</td>\n<td>Procurement-grade evaluation source</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Singh et al., \"The Leaderboard Illusion\" (re Chatbot Arena)</td>\n<td>2025</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20879</td>\n<td>Private testing/data asymmetries distort Arena rankings</td>\n<td>Don't select models on leaderboards alone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chang et al., \"A Survey on Evaluation of Large Language Models\" (ACM TIST)</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.03109</td>\n<td>Comprehensive evaluation-method survey</td>\n<td>Orientation text for evaluation domain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weidinger et al., \"Toward an Evaluation Science for Generative AI Systems\"</td>\n<td>2025</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.05336</td>\n<td>Argues for safety-engineering-style eval maturity</td>\n<td>FAIAS's meta-position on evaluation, well argued</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ganguli et al., \"Challenges in evaluating AI systems\" (Anthropic essay)</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://www.anthropic.com/research/evaluating-ai-systems</td>\n<td>Practical catalogue of why evals mislead</td>\n<td>Caveats register for any assurance report (a previously conflated Apollo/UK AISI half-row removed 26 Aug 2026; AISI material lives in the regulatory register)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Zheng, Chiang, Sheng et al., \"Judging LLM-as-a-Judge with MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.05685</td>\n<td>Identifies position, verbosity and self-enhancement bias in LLM judges; validates human-agreement methodology; GPT-4 judges achieve &gt;80% human agreement</td>\n<td>MOD-015's sole methodology anchor — human-agreement study design, bias-probe construction (order-swap, length-match), correlated-error compensating control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Xu, X., \"The Oversight Gap: What LLM Safety Monitors Miss, and Why It Is Not Capability\" (Carnegie Mellon University)</td>\n<td>2026</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.07162</td>\n<td>Several properties safety monitors are asked to certify — cross-tenant noninterference, sandbagging, evaluation awareness — are 2-safety hyperproperties witnessed only by two executions; a tight bound puts the balanced accuracy of <strong>any</strong> single-trace monitor at ½ + ½·TV(P₀,P₁), replacing binary undecidability with a graded \"oversight gap\"</td>\n<td>Turns \"a single trace cannot decide this\" into a measurable shortfall, which is what deliberate gap 2 needs to be argued rather than asserted; the reported replication on exploited production code (single trace 50.4%, paired traces 61.4%, pair plus a comparison rule 91.1%, across seven models; Technical Appendix) says a monitor-validity test must be designed over trace <em>pairs</em>. Preprint, cs.LG/cs.CR, v1 7 Sep 2026, sole author, <strong>not peer-reviewed</strong>; the paper's Technical Appendix states a code release — \"Every table is regenerated by the named script in the code release\" — but <strong>no URL is printed anywhere in the v1 PDF</strong>, so no public code or data location is available; not FS-specific</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Heidari, Memarian &amp; Rabusseau, \"Evaluation Awareness in Language Models: Representation, Verbalization, and Control\"</td>\n<td>2026</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.21766</td>\n<td>\"evaluation awareness is linearly decodable from the residual streams of every model (best AUROC ≥ 0.7)\"; the representations \"align only in part with verbalization\"; steering along probe-derived directions <strong>\"can shift\"</strong> verbalisation scores; awareness \"is already present within base models, becomes amplified throughout the stages of supervised fine-tuning, and remains stable thereafter\"</td>\n<td>The source that breaks verbalisation-only evaluation-awareness measurement: a firm scoring what a model <em>says</em> about being tested is measuring a partial proxy for what it represents. Bears directly on deliberate gap 2 and on any awareness-stratified re-analysis. Preprint, cs.CL/cs.AI, v1 22 Aug 2026, <strong>not peer-reviewed</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chen, J., Chen, W., Lin, Z. &amp; Vong, C.M., \"A Judge Should Know What Changed: Construct Validity for LLM-as-a-Judge Evaluation\"</td>\n<td>2026</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.24419</td>\n<td>Construct validity is put as a two-dimensional profile — \"invariance S, the probability that a verdict is unchanged under construct-preserving edits, and construct sensitivity R, the probability that it changes under minimal construct-changing edits\"; \"At matched invariance S &gt;= 0.90, judges average S = 0.945 but R = 0.319\", so a judge can be highly stable and still barely track the construct it is scoring; and \"surface-only predictors reproduce 55%-67% of labels in paired mode\", including 67.4% of MT-Bench human votes</td>\n<td>A direct methodological challenge to MOD-015's §11 core test, not a corroboration of it: the existing test measures judge/human agreement (Zheng et al. 2023), and this paper shows stability under paraphrase is not sensitivity to the construct. The 67.4% figure lands on the Zheng et al. anchor row this one sits beside. Pairs with Hallgren 2012, which makes the same point in classical psychometric terms. <strong>Two things follow, both HELD for the owner:</strong> whether §11 gains a sensitivity/invariance probe alongside the agreement check, and whether that moves the ★3. Preprint, cs.AI, v1 25 Aug 2026, 39 pages, <strong>not peer-reviewed</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>van der Weij, T., Hofstätter, F., Jaffe, O., Brown, S.F. &amp; Ward, F.R., \"AI Sandbagging: Language Models can Strategically Underperform on Evaluations\"</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.07358</td>\n<td>\"models can be fine-tuned, on a synthetic dataset, to hide specific capabilities unless given a password\"; \"This behaviour generalizes to high-quality, held-out benchmarks such as WMDP\"; conclusion: \"capability evaluations are vulnerable to sandbagging\"</td>\n<td>The published methodology for elicitation adequacy — the protocol side of a gap the standard otherwise records as \"none established\" for deployer-side <em>detection</em>. OpenAI adapts a related design in its own system card (§9.2.2.3.1). Preprint, cs.AI/cs.CL/cs.CY/cs.LG, v1 11 Jun 2024, v4 6 Feb 2025; <strong>no venue or journal reference stated on the arXiv abstract page (checked 2026-09-12)</strong> — the paper is old enough to have been published since, and the venue cross-check was rate-limited, so peer-review status is recorded as unstated rather than negative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Needham, J., Edkins, G., Pimpale, G., Bartsch, H. &amp; Hobbhahn, M., \"Large Language Models Often Know When They Are Being Evaluated\"</td>\n<td>2025</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.23836</td>\n<td>A benchmark of \"1,000 prompts and transcripts from 61 distinct datasets\"; \"Gemini-2.5-Pro reaches an AUC of 0.83\" against a \"simple human baseline (AUC of 0.92)\"; \"both AI models and humans are better at identifying evaluations in agentic settings compared to chat settings\"</td>\n<td>The one published, reusable classification benchmark for evaluation awareness — the measurement instrument deliberate gap 2's premise has lacked. The agentic-vs-chat asymmetry matters for FS: agentic deployments are where awareness is most detectable by the model and least detectable by the reviewer. Preprint, cs.CL/cs.AI, v1 28 May 2025, v3 16 Jul 2025; <strong>no venue or journal reference stated on the arXiv abstract page (checked 2026-09-12)</strong> — recorded as unstated rather than negative, as with the other rows whose venue the abstract page does not state</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hallgren, K.A., \"Computing Inter-Rater Reliability for Observational Data: An Overview and Tutorial\" (<em>Tutorials in Quantitative Methods for Psychology</em> 8(1), 23–34; DOI 10.20982/tqmp.08.1.p023)</td>\n<td>2012</td>\n<td>https://www.tqmp.org/RegularArticles/vol08-1/p023/p023.pdf</td>\n<td>\"IRR analysis is distinct from validity analysis, which assesses how closely an instrument measures an actual construct rather than how well coders provide similar ratings\" (p.24); the paper's own mechanism for the gap, p.24: \"an instrument may have good IRR but poor validity if coders' scores are highly similar and have a large shared variance but the instrument does not properly represent the construct it is intended to measure\"</td>\n<td>The citable authority for a distinction the standard relies on in three places — MOD-015's judge/human agreement, HUM-001's disagreement-rate monitoring and the DAT annotator-IRR procedure: <strong>agreement between reviewers is a reliability measure and never a validity measure.</strong> Peer-reviewed journal article (CONFIRMED — the DOI 10.20982/tqmp.08.1.p023 verified against the Crossref registration record api.crossref.org/works/10.20982/tqmp.08.1.p023, 12 Sep 2026; the DOI is not printed in the PDF)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},{"h":"13. Cross-cutting surveys & risk taxonomies","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Citation</th>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Link</th>\n<th>1-line finding</th>\n<th>Assurance relevance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Weidinger et al., \"Taxonomy of Risks Posed by Language Models\" (FAccT)</td>\n<td>2022</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.1145/3531146.3533088</td>\n<td>21-risk, 6-area LM harm taxonomy</td>\n<td>Backbone taxonomy many frameworks inherit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Slattery et al., \"The AI Risk Repository\" (MIT)</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.12622</td>\n<td>Living database unifying 700+ risks from 40+ taxonomies</td>\n<td>Completeness check for FAIAS domain coverage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bengio et al., \"International AI Safety Report\"</td>\n<td>Jan 2025 (+2026 update)</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.17805</td>\n<td>Consensus scientific synthesis of frontier-AI risk evidence</td>\n<td>Authoritative citation for senior stakeholders</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hendrycks et al., \"An Overview of Catastrophic AI Risks\"</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.12001</td>\n<td>Four-source catastrophic risk framing</td>\n<td>Context for proportionality arguments</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shevlane et al. — see DeepMind extreme-risk evals (listed in labs register)</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.15324</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications (2025)</td>\n<td>2025</td>\n<td>https://owasp.org/www-project-top-10-for-large-language-model-applications/</td>\n<td>Practitioner vulnerability top-10 incl. injection, poisoning</td>\n<td>De facto control checklist in FS security reviews</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>NIST AI 100-2e2025, \"Adversarial Machine Learning: Taxonomy and Terminology\"</td>\n<td>Mar 2025</td>\n<td>https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.AI.100-2e2025</td>\n<td>Authoritative AML taxonomy (evasion, poisoning, privacy, misuse)</td>\n<td>Regulator-friendly terminology anchor</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},{"h":"14. Safety & hazard benchmarking","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Citation</th>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Link</th>\n<th>1-line finding</th>\n<th>Assurance relevance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Ghosh et al. (MLCommons, 102 co-authors), \"AILuminate: Introducing v1.0 of the AI Risk and Reliability Benchmark from MLCommons\"</td>\n<td>2025</td>\n<td>https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.05731</td>\n<td>Consortium-governed, versioned prompt battery; five-tier (Poor–Excellent) grading across 12 hazard categories; entropy-based system-response evaluation</td>\n<td>Candidate methodology for CON-009's ordinary-use harm-taxonomy testing and a regression comparator for SEC-001's per-version jailbreak re-testing — not FS-certified, single-turn and English-only in v1.0</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}]};
